## Contents
## 1. Introduction
The development of advanced and flexible devices for energy storage and conversion has become an important topic of research in the modern world due to their broad uses. Such devices can include domestic photovoltaic (PV) installers with a few kW capacity to very large pumped hydropower with several hundred MW capacity. Also, the duration of energy storage encompasses very short-frequency response services (e.g., grid operators) to very long ones such as seasonal storage (e.g., hydro reservoirs) [1]. The primary components of energy storage devices are batteries due to their easy fabrication and operation. Recent advances in energy research have resulted in the fabrication of novel batteries with enhanced energy storage capabilities. At present, approximately 25,000 domestic installations are operational in Germany alone in conjunction with PV installations with a total capacity of 160 MWh [2]. According to a report by the World Energy Resources, the net capacity of batteries in electric vehicles is growing rapidly and is estimated to reach up to $250\mathsf{G W}$ by 2030. Another report by Green Tech Media (GTM) states that the price of energy storage devices will fall by approximately $40\%$ by 2020. Further, investment banks report that costs of the LIBs will fall due to progress in renewable technologies [3].
The fundamental purpose of battery systems is to convert electrical energy into electrochemical energy (and vice-versa) on demand. The advancements in renewable energy technologies (e.g., solar cells and wind energy) are expanding very rapidly. However, the extent of energy to be produced and/or stored by these resources is expected to be immense. To meet such goals and demands, enormous efforts have been made to expand energy storage and conversion technologies. For instance, many research groups are aiming to increase the capacitance level of batteries while simultaneously lowering their cost to improve the efficiency of energy storage systems [4–8].
Lithium is one of the lightest elements in the periodic table, and it has very high electrochemical potentials. The unique physicochemical properties of Li ions provide some of the highest possible voltages in the most compact and lightest volumes. LIBs were commercialized worldwide starting from the late $90^{\prime}s$ . LIBs are widely used in mobile phones, computers, and portable electronic devices along with ecofriendly electric/hybrid vehicles [9–14]. LIBs are receiving increased attention due to their high energy density and low weight compared to the conventional lead acid batteries and Ni-Cd batteries. Similarly, $\operatorname{Li}-0_{2}$ and Li-S batteries with high theoretical energy density (approximately 5 to 10 times higher than the LIBs) also exhibit the potential to be chosen for next generation energy storage devices. According to a report, Tesla’s Giga factory will reach a full capacity of 35 GWh per year by 2020 and is expected to produce more LIBs annually than were produced worldwide in 2013 [15]. However, some practical shortcomings associated with cycle stability and side product formation restrict the real-world applications of Li- ${{\bf{\cdot}}}_{}0_{2}$ and Li-S batteries [16–18].
The advancement of energy storage systems primarily revolves around the development of efficient electrode materials. An ideal electrode material should be highly porous and chemically/ thermally stable, while possessing excellent ionic/electrical conductivity and suitable lattice sites to reversibly store lithium ions [19–21]. To date, different types of metal oxides and composites (e.g., mesoporous carbon, carbon fiber reinforced plastic, silicon-carbon systems, and graphite-metal oxides) have been used to enhance the efficiency of batteries [22–28]. However, their poor conductivity and deformation upon charging/discharging result in low battery life and low efficiency. On the other hand, many carbon-based materials (such as graphene and carbon nanotubes (CNTs)) with large pore volumes and inherent conductivity are recognized as attractive candidates for efficient electrode materials with their excellent chemical and mechanical stability [29–32]. Nonetheless, the current synthesis protocols are either too expensive or do not allow for the realization of specific structures with desirable qualities of surface areas and pore dimensions. These are critical issues in extending their applications to large-scale installations of energy storage devices.
The intriguing characteristics (e.g., controllable morphology, abundant pores, and multi-functional sites) of metal organic frameworks (MOFs) have been recognized based on the advent of their synthesis techniques [33]. Hence, MOFs have been preferably employed in diverse applications such as gas storage, separation, purification [34–37], solar cells [38], drug delivery [39–41], catalysis [42–46], sensing [47,48], thin film systems [49,50], proton conduction [51–54], renewable energy [55] and many other miscellaneous fields [56–60]. Likewise, MOFs have also been proposed as promising candidates for the development of next generation electrode materials [27,61–64]. By choosing the specific metal ions and tailoring the pore size using appropriate organic linkers (e.g., amides, carboxylates, and other groups), one can customize the MOF properties in line with the desired application. In addition, MOF-based nanostructures and/or nanocomposites made of metal oxides and nanoporous carbon have been known for having synergistic properties while retaining the well-defined structure of the MOF.
From the perspective of battery technology, due to their micro and/or mesopores nature, MOFs provide effective sites for lithiation/delithiation process to help realize efficient charge storage. A large number of MOFs are synthesized utilizing ligands containing carboxylic group such as BDC and BTC. These types of ligands are particularly reactive towards lithium ions. Likewise, it is meaningful to exploit other potential linkers, e.g., benzene hexacarboxylic acid (BHC), which bear large numbers of carboxylic groups to possibly offer even higher battery efficiency. MOFs are stable structure on account of coordinative bonds to efficiently tolerate volume expansion and contraction during charging/discharging reactions in LIBs [65]. The metal ions and organic linkers in the MOF structure are also known to chemical interaction (e.g., Lewis acid/base interaction with polysulfide) with discharged products to help in improving the stability and cycle performance of LIBs [66–68]. A porous structure of MOFs also makes them potential host materials for different dopants [69–71]. Furthermore, MOFs that contain nitrogen (e.g., ZIF-8) are suitable precursors to obtain nitrogen doped carbon materials through a simple process of pyrolysis [28]. Utilizing this approach, carbon materials with around 17 wt% N have been successfully synthesized to carry out important electrode applications in LIBs [72,73]. The tunable porosity of MOFs is reported highly useful in Li-air battery applications wherein the optimization of pores could be highly beneficial in ensuring the supply of pure oxygen [74,75]. At times, the hydrophobic nature of organic linkers is advantageous to sufficiently resist the entry of water molecules into the battery [76– 79]. Moreover, the active metal sites in the MOF structure can act as catalytic centers to increase the efficiency of Li-air batteries by breaking down oxygen molecules [80]. MOFs can also act as separators by holding lithium-based ionic liquids in their pores using their plentiful polar surface functional groups. In addition, the adhesive interaction with ${\mathrm{Li}}^{+}$ ions can enhance the uptake capability of electrolyte and the flux of well distributed homogeneous electrolyte ions [81]. The above-mentioned properties of MOFs and their composites make them attractive candidates for the development of novel energy storage systems.
This review focuses on the recent advancements in MOF-based electrode materials for Li-based energy storage devices (LESD). We elucidate the possible routes to modify the MOF structure to enhance their capacity and stability. The early materials employed for LIBs were also discussed along with recent updates on MOFs. The need to modify pristine MOFs for better performance has also been discussed in detail. Additionally, this review article expounds on the evolution of high-end ${\mathrm{Li}}^{+}$ ion batteries (Li-S and $\mathrm{LiO}_{2}$ ), which are expected to exhibit tremendous theoretical capacity despite the yet unrealized performance under practical conditions. Intriguing insights into the performance parameters (e.g., capacity, internal resistance, and chemical kinetics) of MOF-based systems have also been discussed. The main aim of this review article is to provide a roadmap for the development of highly efficient LESDs by identifying the present shortcomings and obstacles in this challenging area of research. Moreover, possible solutions have also been discussed to help expand the present understanding of the relationship between physiochemical characteristics of MOFbased structures and their expected performance as energy storage devices (e.g., in terms of overall efficiency, energy density, and power density) to make them more stable against side products and reversibility. To the best of our knowledge, this is the only review article that comprehensively deals with the application of MOFs, their derived structures, and composites for all the available lithium batteries.
## 2. Metal organic frameworks (MOFs) and their derived structures for Li-ion batteries
At present, rechargeable LIBs exhibit great potential for electric vehicles, grid energy storage, and renewable energy storage. Commercial LIBs are composed of two electrodes, i.e., the cathode and anode. Cathodes are usually made of Li transition metal oxide (e.g. $\operatorname{LiCoO}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{LiMn}_{2}{\sf O}_{4}$ , and ${\mathrm{LiFePO}}_{4}{\mathrm{.}}$ ), while anodes are made of carbonaceous materials (e.g. activated carbon) [10,82–84]. Both the materials are generally layered in nature so that the lithium ions can easily move through the material. Usually, LIBs are assembled with discharged materials separated by a non-aqueous electrolyte (mostly organic carbonates) due to the high reactivity of Li species in the presence of water. However, the ionic conductivity of these electrolytes is usually very low, resulting in small separation between the electrodes. During the first charging of the battery, around $5\mathrm{-}20\%$ lithium is consumed in the formation of a solid electrolyte interface (SEI) at the anodic surface [85]. However, in the preceding cycles, a small percentage of lithium is consumed for SEI. This SEI layer is composed of highly dense inorganic compounds $_{\mathrm{Li}_{2}0}$ , $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{CO}_{3}$ , and LiF) near the anodic surface and mixed species of organic and inorganic compounds $(\operatorname{Li}_{2}\mathbf{CO}_{3}$ and lithium alkyl carbonate $(\mathsf{R O C O}_{2}\mathrm{Li})$ ) on the electrolyte side [85]. The presence of SEI in LIBs is critical due to its uncontrolled growth. Nevertheless, the bright side of the SEI layer formation is that they protect batteries during overpotential and prevent material decomposition [86,87].
The first LIB models were proposed by Whittingham in 1970 with titanium (IV) sulfide and lithium metal as cathode and anode materials, respectively [88]. In 1973, Adam Heller proposed the lithium thionyl chloride battery, made of metallic lithium as an anode and liquid thionyl chloride $({\mathsf{S o C l}}_{2})$ as a cathode, this device showed long life, high energy density, and an extreme operating temperature of $-55^{\circ}C$ [89]. However, the electrochemistry of these batteries was not reversible, and the electrolyte employed was highly toxic in nature with a high propensity to react with water [90]. In addition, high cost and safety concerns (battery explosion upon shortening) severely limited their practical applications. In 1981, two scientists, Jhon Goodenough and Kaichi Mizushima, demonstrated a rechargeable cell with a voltage in $3.2\mathrm{V}$ range using lithium cobalt oxide $\begin{array}{r}{(\operatorname{LiCoO}_{2})}\end{array}$ ) as a cathode (modified through the incorporation of additives) and lithium metal as an anode material [91]. However, the use of a lithium metal anode was limited due to the serious drawback of lithium dendrite formation on the anode surface (during charging). The dendrites refer to the growth of needle-like metal particles on the anode surface. This innovation helped establish graphite as an important anode material for LIBs that can effectively replace lithium metal. In 1983, Basu et al. developed a working lithium intercalated graphite electrode at Bell Labs to provide an alternative to the anode materials commonly used in LIBs [92]. In 1983, Thackeray also identified manganese spinel as an alternate positive material to replace $\mathsf{L i C o O}_{2}$ (high market cost) [93]. In 1989, Goodenough and coworkers showed that a cathode material containing polyanions, e.g., sulfites $(\mathsf{S O}_{3})^{2-}$ , could be used to produce much higher voltages than oxides due to the induction effect of the polyanions [94]. Finally, in 1990, Sony Corporation released the first commercial LIB in which lithium cobalt oxide and graphite were used as cathode and anode materials, respectively [95]. However, the poor cyclability and high cost of $\mathrm{LiCoO}_{2}$ propelled the need for better alternatives. In 1996, Padhi and co-workers proposed lithium iron phosphate $\left(\mathrm{LiFePO}_{4}\right)$ and other phospho-olivine materials as a cathode for LIBs [82]. However, the poor conductivity of oxides was a restriction, which resulted in the loss of columbic efficiency of the battery. In 2002, Chiang and his group boosted the n-type conductivity of the analyzed materials through supervalent cation doping $(\mathrm{Mg}^{2+}$ , $A|^{3+}$ , ${\mathrm{Ti}}^{4+}$ , ${\mathrm{Zr}}^{4+}$ , ${\mathsf{N b}}^{5+}$ or $\mathsf{W}^{6+}$ ), which effectively enhanced the overall performance [96].
Battery performance also depends on the material morphology and particle size. In 2004, Chiang demonstrated that iron phosphate with a particle size of ${<}100\ \mathrm{nm}$ can effectively increase the performance of batteries [97]. The cause of the enhanced performance of LIBs was attributed to the decreased particle density and increased specific surface area. In 2007, Chan et al. developed silicon nanowire-based anodes for LIBs [98]. This silicon nanowire can accommodate large strain without pulverization on volume changes $(400\%)$ during charging/discharging. Further, in 2012, these nanowires were doped with impurities to improve their electrochemistry and show enhanced performance [99]. Still, the need for further upgrading LIBs persists due to the very common problems of volume expansion and the loss of original electrode structure during lithiation/delithiation. Severe volume changes tend to occur during the lithiation/delithiation process, which can further change the morphology of the active material. These consequences lead to large irreversible capacity loss and poor capacity retention. To date, different oxides and carbon-based materials have been used as anode materials for LIBs including: nitrogen-doped/ graphitic carbon [100], metal oxides (NiO [101], $\mathsf{M o O}_{3}$ [102], $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ [103], $\mathsf{V S}_{2}$ [104], $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ [105], $\mathsf{S n O}_{2}$ [106,107], $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ [108]), $\mathsf{S n S}_{2}$ [109], $\ensuremath{\mathrm{MnO}_{2}}$ [110], $\mathsf{C o}_{9}\mathsf{S}_{8}$ [111], metal sulfides $(\mathsf{M o S}_{2}$ [112]), and lithium alloys (Sn, Si, Sb)/multinary alloys [113–115]. However, most of these suffer from poor electrical conductivity, low structural stability, and serious electrode pulverization due to large volume expansion/contraction of the materials during charging/ discharging.
## 2.1. Pristine MOFs as an anode material for Li-ion batteries
The first possible application of MOFs as an electrode material for LIBs was proposed by Li et al. in 2006 [116]. These authors synthesized a polycrystalline MOF-177 $\langle Z\mathsf{n}_{4}0(1,3,5-$ benzenetribenzoate $)_{2}$ ) in different morphologies and proposed its application in lithium storage devices. They evaluated the possibility of shape-controlled synthesis by varying reaction conditions such as temperature, time, reactant concentration, molar ratio of $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ to $\mathrm{\Delta}\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{BTB}$ , and the heating/cooling rates. However, the electrochemical results revealed the poor performance of MOF-177 for applications in reversible lithium storage. The specific capacity of
MOF-177 in the second discharge process was only $105\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , down from $400\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ recorded in the first cycle [116].
From a morphological perspective, microcubes delivered relatively high charge/discharge capacities as compared to microcuboids. This is because the prepared MOF-177 microcubes are dispersive in nature, whereas the micro-cuboids are aggregated to each other. As the dispersed form favors contact between the active material and electrolyte, the utilization efficiency of microcubes is higher than that of micro-cuboids [116]. Although the electrochemical performance of MOF-177 was not attractive, the high storage capability of such porous MOFs was expected due to the numerous reaction sites and structural advantages. In 2009, Combarieu et al. showed the first ever evidence of reversible lithium storage using MOFs (MIL-53(Fe)) [117]. This Fe-MOF displayed an enhanced electrochemical reactivity because the lithium ion $(\mathrm{Li^{+}})$ was inserted into the structure of Fe-MOF due to the oxidation of $\mathsf{F e}^{2+}$ to $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ [62,118,119]. However, the limited number of lithium insertions $\mathrm{(0.6Li^{+}}$ per $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ without any significant structural changes) and low conductivity of MIL-53(Fe) limited its capacity to $68\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{h}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . To overcome these limits, a redox mediator (1,4-benzoquinone) was adsorbed in the MIL-53 (Fe) structure, and it enhanced the electron transfer (theoretical acceptance of $2\mathsf{e}^{-}$ per molecule). During the discharge, the quinone molecules were reduced to quinolate species, which are non-soluble in electrolyte, to maintain the crystal lattice. However, the quinone molecules became prone to dissolution with the loss of quinine molecules during the charging cycle [117]. This led to a capacity degradation from $95\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{h}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ to $68\mathrm{\mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ after only five cycles. In another report, Han et al. (2012) synthesized metal-1,4,5,8- naphthalenetetracarboxylates (metal ${\bf\varepsilon}=\mathrm{Li}$ and/or Ni) through a rheological phase reaction to achieve high capacity due to the capability of NTC to intercalate $\mathrm{18Li^{+}}$ per unit formulae [120]. These authors mixed Li-NTC and Ni-NTC in a 1:1 ratio to obtain Li/Ni-NTC. For comparison, they also analyzed the electrochemical performances of Li-NTC and Ni-NTC individually as well. Their studies revealed that Ni-NTC exhibited good electrical conductivity due to the presence of unpaired electrons and was found to deliver good initial capacity of $1823\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . However, due to the lack of stable structure, its capacity degraded to $238\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ after 80 charging/discharging cycles. On the other hand, Li-NTC exhibited good structural stability with limited capacity. Interestingly, the synergistic effect of both Li and Ni with NTC delivered the initial discharge capacity of $1084\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , which was reduced to $475\mathrm{\mAh\g^{-1}}$ after 80 charging/discharging cycles (Fig. 1) [120]. The use of multiple metal component in MOF promote electronic delocalization in the material thus increasing the electronic conductivity of the overall material. However, for this there is a need of 3D structure so that the metals can interact with each other for electronic localization [121–123]. Recently, Zhou et al. (2019) have directly grown the 3D nanorod of MOF-74 (Fe, Co and Ni) on the nickel foam without any need of binder. This tri-metal MOF delivered a capacity of $834\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at the current density of $50\mathrm{mAg}^{-1}$ which remained unchanged $(93\%)$ up to 500 charging/discharging cycles at $1\mathsf{A}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ [124]. Most MOFs are not stable under moist conditions due to framework collapse. Nonetheless, the harsh conditions of electrochemical environments demand extremely stable MOF structures. In view of this, Lin et al. (2014) modified the MOF structure through the addition of hydrophobic functionalities via post-synthetic modifications [125]. In addition, the inner pores of the MOF were functionalized through the chemical grafting of polar groups, which can interact with the guest molecules. The resulting structure was referred to as bi-functionalized MOF (BMOF) with both hydrophobic and polar groups. The prepared BMOF showed a reversible capacity of $190\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ even after 100 charging/ discharging cycles. A coulombic efficiency of near $100\%$ was achieved with a very high current density of $400\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ even after 200 cycles [125]
Generally, in lithiation/de-lithiation processes, the transition metal participates in the conversion mechanism, which leads to the destruction of the MOF framework. On the other hand, intercalation or insertion mechanisms lead to the destruction or change in the structure of the active material. To push back these limitations, the usage of organic linkers with the rigid inorganic cluster can be devised to offer redox active properties (so that ${\mathrm{Li}}^{+}$ can easily reduce the metal species) with a high degree of flexibility [126]. In this hybrid structure, $\mathrm{Li}^{+}$ will be stored by reducing and replacing the base metal linker of the MOF instead of being stored in the pores of the MOF. Unfortunately, this mechanism of lithium storage generally destroyed the MOF structure during cycling. However, the storage of lithium through redox reactions in flexible organic cluster may be helpful to restrain the structure modulation during charging/discharging. Therefore, the selection of a suitable organic linker is very crucial for the practical applicability of MOF materials as electrodes in LIBs. Interestingly, carboxylates with weak electron withdrawing capabilities could actively participate in redox reactions to coordinate with ${\mathrm{Li}}^{+}$ ions, which makes them eligible as candidates for MOF linkers [83,127]. In the last few years, 1,4-benzenedicarboxylate (BDC) has received great attention as an organic linker due to the ease of availability and stabilization with lots of metals. Gou et al. synthesized a Co-MOF with BDC linkers as an anode material for LIBs [128]. Co-MOF showed a rectangular-parallelepiped morphology. The Co-MOF can store up to $\mathrm{16Li^{+}}$ ions to show good electrochemical performance. This MOF delivered a first discharge and charge capacity of 1385 and $1005\mathrm{mAhg^{-1}}$ , respectively, with a coulombic efficiency of $72.8\%$ . The efficiency was observed to decrease in the next few cycles and was observed to stabilize at $650\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ after 100 charging/discharging cycles [128]. Recently, Xia et al. (2019) have carried out the DFT (density functional theory) calculations to demonstrate reversible reaction of $\mathrm{Li}^{+}$ with carboxylate ions [129]. These authors found that carboxylate ions can provide channels and binding sites for the rapid diffusion of $\mathrm{Li^{+}}$ . Oxygen atoms (hard base) of carboxylate ions were cited to possess a good affinity toward Ti(IV) ions, which described the exceptional stability of TiMOF with carboxylate ions. The above sample demonstrated a reversible capacity of $527.12\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{hg}^{-1}$ after 100 cycles at the current density of $0.1\mathrm{Ag}^{-1}$ [129]. In another similar work, Tb-MOF was demonstrated to deliver a specific capacity of $395.2\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{hg}^{-1}$ after 100 cycles of charring/discharging at the rate of $100\mathrm{mAg}^{-1}$ [129].
Along similar lines, 1,3,5-benzenetricarboxylate (BTC)-based Mn-BTC was capable of generating 18 possible sites for Li insertion due to the presence of stronger $\pi{-}\pi$ interactions for the stabilization of the 3D MOF framework [126]. As an anode for electrochemical performance, Mn-BTC showed the highest capacity of $694\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at a corresponding current density of $103\mathrm{\mAg}^{-1}$ with $83\%$ of capacity retention after 100 charging/discharging cycles (Fig. 2) [126]. Recently, a layered Ni-based MOF $\langle\mathsf{N i}_{2}(\mathsf{H}_{2^{-}}$ $\mathbf{0})_{10}]$ $[\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}(\mathrm{C}_{12}\mathrm{O}_{12})]{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})$ featuring a fast kinetic structure and ultrahigh capacity was synthesized using 1,2,3,4,5,6-benzene hexacarboxylic acid (BHC) with six carboxylate groups [130]. The presence of water and coordinated solvent molecules in the electrode material can create serious problems such as: (i) the formation of irreversible products $\mathrm{LiO}_{2}$ and LiOH), (ii) the production of HF upon interaction with organic electrolyte, leading to the destruction of the electrode, and (iii) the reduction of lithiation sites due to the presence of large amounts of coordinated solvent molecules. To resolve these issues, these authors dehydrated the Ni-BHC structure by evacuating the sample at $220^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $^{3\mathrm{h}}$ in air. As an anode, Ni-BHC delivered an excellent reversible capacity of $1261.3\mathrm{mAhg}^{-1}$ at $100\mathrm{mAg}^{-1}$ even after 50 charging/discharging cycles. Thus, the presence of carboxylate groups free from coordination with solvent molecules might lead to an enhanced energy density with superior cyclability in LIBs [130].
Apart from the usage of MOFs as electrodes, MOF-525(Cu) was employed as a solid electrolyte by impregnating lithium based ionic liquid (LiIL) in the pores [131]. Interestingly, the encapsulated ILs lost their liquid characteristics but retained their ionic conductivity to help migrate the ${\mathrm{Li}}^{+}$ ions. Moreover, to improve the transport of ${\mathrm{Li}}^{+}$ ions throughout the cathode, these authors mixed $\mathsf{L i F e P O}_{4}$ with LiIL ${\mathcal{O}}\mathbf{M}\mathbf{O}\mathbf{F}$ to acquire high ionic conductivity as well as nano-wetted interfaces in the cathode. On electrochemical testing, it recorded an initial discharge capacity of $145\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , which slightly decreased to $135\mathrm{\mAh\g^{-1}}$ after 100 charging/discharging cycles (Fig. 2) [131]. Further, some other MOFs, including Co-BTC [132], $\mathrm{{Li}_{2}(O B A)}$ $\langle\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{OBA}=4,4^{\prime}$ -oxybisbenzoicacid) [133] and Prussian blue analogs $(\mathrm{M}_{3}(\mathrm{II})[\mathsf{C o}(\mathrm{III})(\mathsf{C N})_{6}]_{2}{\cdot}\mathsf{n H}_{2}0$ ( $\mathbf{\dot{M}}=\mathbf{\dot{C}}\mathbf{0}$ , Mn)) [134], have also been studied as anode materials for LIBs. These tested MOFs generally exhibited high reversible capacity and good performance as potential anode materials. Detailed descriptions of pristine MOFs along with their performance parameters when used in LIBs are provided in Table 1.
2.2. MOF derived nanoporous carbons and metal oxides as an anode material for Li-ion batteries
Metal oxides are touted as promising materials to effectively replace graphite in LIBs. The primary advantage offered by the metal oxides is their chemical transformation during charging wherein they exchange one or more electrons instead of intercalating lithium ions. Moreover, the low lithiation potential of metal oxides makes them a safer option to limit lithium dendrite formation on charging [149]. However, the selection of MOF is highly challenging these days. The different factors relating to MOF such as its ligands, metal nodes, morphology, synthesis process and size can greatly affect the performance of the LIBs [150]. A Cu-MOF derived CuO nanostructure is also an alternative anode material for LIBs to replace graphite [151–155]. A Cu-MOF derived $\mathtt{C u O}$ electrode was tested for the electrochemical properties in a half-cell assembly, and it showed an initial charge density of $1208\mathrm{mAhg^{-1}}$ . Cyclic voltammetry (CV) analysis revealed that $\mathord{\sim}1.6\mathrm{mol}$ of Li $(\sim538\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ is cyclable via a conversion reaction. Also, it delivered a reversible capacity of ${\sim}210\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{h}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ with a high current rate of $2\mathsf{A}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ . Moreover, the Cu-MOF derived $\mathtt{C u O}$ anode retained $\sim90\%$ of its initial reversible capacity after 40 cycles, which can be attributed to its morphological features [155]. In another report, porous $\mathtt{C u O}$ hollow architectures with perfect octahedral morphologies were synthesized by simply annealing $\left[\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{b t c})_{2}\right]$ MOF (btc $\mathbf{\sigma}=\mathbf{\sigma}$ benzene1,3,5-tricarboxylate) [156]. The anodic material was observed to have a first discharge specific capacity of $1294~\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{hg}^{-1}$ at a current density of $100\mathrm{mAg}^{-1}$ . At a very high current density of $2\mathsf{A}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ , it yielded a specific capacity of $201.9\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . For the initial charge/discharge cycle, the coulombic efficiency was quite low $(40\%)$ but increased to $99\%$ after a few charge/discharge cycles, probably through the generation of a SEI layer [156]. The formed layer resulted in the loss of lithium atoms due to the formation of irreversible lithium byproducts (lithium hydroxides, oxides, and carbonates) in the first few cycles [85,157,158]
Manganese oxides have also attracted interest due to their highly favorable potential in energy applications such as water oxidation and oxygen reduction [83–85], electrochemical capacitors [86,87], and LIBs [88–97]. Their favorable features include high theoretical specific capacity, low cost, eco-friendly nature, low operating voltages $(1.3\mathrm{-}1.5\mathrm{V}$ for lithium extraction), and small voltage hysteresis $(<0.8\:\mathrm{V})$ [89,91,100]. These advantageous properties make manganese oxides $\mathrm{{.{MnO_{x},}}}$ $1\leq\mathbf{x}\leq2$ ) the most promising alternative for anode materials in the construction of the next generation LIBs [93,97–99]. Likewise, Mn-MOF derived $\mathsf{M n O}_{\mathrm{x}}$ nanostructures were also tested as anode materials for LIB applications [159]. These authors prepared polyhedron $\scriptstyle\mathrm{{Mn}}_{2}\mathrm{{O}}_{3}$ with a small interior cavity, which was observed to deliver a high specific capacity of $819.8\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at a current density of $1\mathsf{A}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ after
1200 cycles. When tested at a high current density of $2\mathsf{A}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ , an excellent specific capacity of $760\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ was still observable even after 1000 charge/discharge cycles. The observed high stability can be attributed to the ability of a hollow polyhedron structure to accommodate the inward volume expansion/ contraction during charging/discharging cycles [159]. In addition, porous $\scriptstyle\mathrm{{Mn}}_{2}\mathrm{{O}}_{3}$ from MOF[Mn( $\mathtt{B r}_{4}$ -bdc) (4,40-bpy) $(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O})_{2}\mathsf{l}_{\mathrm{n}}$ $\mathrm{^{\prime}B r_{4}-b d c=}$ tetrabromoterephthalate and $4,4^{\prime}{\-}\mathsf{b p y}=4,4^{\prime}$ -bipyridine) was also synthesized for LIBs [160]. The electrode made of this material delivered initial discharge and charge capacities of $1158\mathrm{mAhg^{-1}}$ and $852\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively, with a columbic efficiency of $74\%$ . Moreover, it retained an excellent reversible capacity of $705\mathrm{mAhg^{-1}}$ after 250 charge/discharge cycles at a current density of $1\mathsf{A}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ [160]. For comparison, micro-sized $\scriptstyle\mathbf{Mn}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{3}$ deliver a specific capacity of $200\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{hg}^{-1}$ , which was mainly attributed to the large volume changes of the $\scriptstyle\mathrm{{Mn}}_{2}\mathrm{{O}}_{3}$ during the lithiation/delithiation process. This led to pulverization of the electrode and poor cyclic stability. All the results suggest the promising potential of hierarchically porous $\scriptstyle\mathrm{{Mn}}_{2}\mathrm{{O}}_{3}$ as an advanced anode material [161–163]. Recently, Gou et al. (2019) have synthesized $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ though the calcination of Co-MOF at different calcining temperature [164]. The as synthesized $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ delivered specific capacity of $1066\mathrm{mAhg^{-1}}$ at a current density of $0.1\mathrm{Ag}^{-1}$ . This enhanced specific capacity was attributed to the presence of mesopores and the small particle size of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ [164].
In another study, octahedral Mn-MIL-100 MOFs were synthesized as templates to fabricate the porous $\ensuremath{\mathrm{Mn}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}}$ octahedra through a post-calcination strategy [165]. They recorded a reversible lithium storage capacity of $755\mathrm{mAhg^{-1}}$ at $_{0.2\mathsf{C}}$ ( $\mathrm{~:~}1\mathrm{~C}=1680\mathrm{mAg^{-1}};$ after performing 100 charge/discharge cycles. The rate capability of porous $\scriptstyle\mathbf{Mn}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{3}$ octahedra was also investigated by galvanostatic charge-discharge tests at various rates ranging from 0.1 to 5 C [165]. Along similar lines, layered 3D $\scriptstyle\mathrm{{Mn}}_{2}\mathrm{{O}}_{3}$ and ${\delta\mathrm{-}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{MnO}_{2}}$ microcuboids were synthesized as an anode material for LIBs [162]. These microcuboids sustained their performance even at very high charging discharging rates. The $\scriptstyle\mathbf{Mn}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{3}$ discharged $437\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at a current density of $2\mathsf{A}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ , while d$\ensuremath{\mathrm{MnO}_{2}}$ discharged $362\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at the same current density. This capacity remained stable up to 400 cycles of charging/discharging [162]. NiO nanoparticles were prepared from Ni-BDC MOF in the size range of $30\mathrm{nm}$ through a combustion-based technique [168]. The prepared samples recorded reversible capacities of $748\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and $410\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{hg}^{-1}$ at current densities of $0.5\mathrm{Ag}^{-1}$ and $1\mathsf{A}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ , respectively, after 100 charge/discharge cycles. The electrode still delivered an appreciable capacity of $200\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ even at a much higher current density of $10\mathsf{A}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ [168].
Apart from the utilization of metal oxides as an anode material, several novel carbon-based anode materials with various structures (e.g., carbon nanotubes $\left(625\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{h}\mathrm{\g}^{-1}\right).$ ) [169], graphene $(\sim568\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{h}\mathrm{g}^{-1};$ [170], and carbon-nanotube/graphene composites (Fig. 4) [166,171]) have been investigated extensively for LIB applications. In addition, partially graphitized porous carbons as a class of emerging materials with promising physicochemical properties (low cost, high conductivity, and porous structure) were also explored for LIB-based applications. In view of these studies, the template-free synthesis of hollow carbon nano-spheres was also carried out to make high-performance anode materials for LIBs, which showed a specific capacity of $630\mathrm{mAhg^{-1}}$ after performing 50 charge/discharge cycles [166]. Similarly, Wang et al. has synthesized hollow carbon spheres with porous silicon core interconnected nanowires. As an anode for LIBs, the above material delivered a reversible specific capacity of $1027\mathrm{mAhg^{-1}}$ after 500 cycles at the current rate of $1\mathsf{A}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ . The combination of carbon shell with silicon core can sufficiently promote the electrical conductivity with faradic reactions and diffusion process [172]. In addition to the porous carbons, nitrogen doped (N-doped) carbon materials are also attractive options for LIBs due to their higher electro-negativity (3.5) as compared to carbon (3.0) coupled with a smaller atomic diameter $(0.22\mathrm{nm})$ [154,173]. Moreover, the N atoms incorporated into graphitic networks efficiently promoted stronger interactions between the N-doped carbon structure and
Li-ions for Li insertion. According to theoretical calculations, the N-doped graphene (with all N atoms contained in the lattice) should have superior electrochemical performance with a specific capacity of about $1262\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ [174]. Theoretical calculations and experimental results have revealed that the $\mathrm{Li}^{+}$ storage capacity for nitrogen-doped material should largely depend on the nitrogen-doping level [166,170,171,173].
However, a large quantity of nitrogen doping made the original structure unstable. It was noted that most N-doped carbon materials for LIBs have a N content of approximately $10\mathrm{{wt\%}}$ [182,183]. Interestingly, the ZIF-8 framework made of the $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ transition metal ion and N-containing 2-methylimidazolate linkers had $34\mathrm{wt\%}$ nitrogen. After pyrolyzing the ZIF-8, the content of Ndoping in graphene particles was $17.72\mathrm{wt\%}$ , which is a substantial increase compared to graphene processed with some other method (as shown in Fig. 4a–d) [175]. The obtained N-doped graphene particles showed a specific capacity of $2132\mathrm{mAhg}^{-1}$ after 50 cycles at a current density of $100\mathrm{mAg}^{-1}$ when used as an anode material. Also, the same anode has retained an excellent specific capacity of $785\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ even after 1000 cycles at a current density of $5\mathsf{A}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ [72]. The remarkable performance of ZIF-8 derived N-doped graphene as an anode material for LIBs can be accounted for by the presence of doped nitrogen within the hexagonal lattices and edges [72]. Hence, material doped with high quantities of nitrogen can be a suitable candidate as an anode material for LIB-based applications. Such nitrogen-enriched materials can be obtained through pyrolysis of MOFs (shown in Fig. 5a–d). Recently Yi et al. (2019) demonstrated the significance of nitrogen doped carbon encapsulated with bi metal (Ni-Co-S) [184]. Ni-Co with $0.5\mathrm{M}$ ratio gave an excellent specific capacity when compared to another molar ratio. The system yielded a specific capacity of $755\mathrm{mAhg^{-1}}$ at the current density of $200\mathrm{\mAg}^{-1}$ for 200 cycles [184].
## 3. MOF composites as anode materials for Li-ion batteries
Composites are a synergistic combination of two or more materials, which exhibit different overall properties as compared to the individual species. However, they also maintain their individuality at the same time [185]. Snail shells (combination of calcium carbonate and proteins) are an example of naturally occurring composite materials [186]. The use of composites dates back to 3000 years when clay was reinforced with straw to build walls [187]. In LIB-based applications, the usage of pristine materials results in some serious shortcomings such as poor conductivity and expansion/contraction of electrodes.
Transition-metal oxides (TMOs) have attracted special attention because their theoretical capacities $(600-1000\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{h}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ are larger than that of the current commercial graphitic anode materials $(\sim372\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{h}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}).$ ) [188]. However, metal oxides often suffer from poor cycling stability and low rate capability due to their poor electrical conductivity and large specific volume changes $(\sim200\%)$ . These drawbacks may lead to cracking and capacity fading of active materials, easy agglomeration of particles, high voltage hysteresis, slow diffusion rate of $\mathrm{Li^{+}}$ , and increased resistance of electrolyte during charging/discharging processes [189–199]. Similarly, carbon-based materials such as CNTs and graphene exhibited much better electrical conductivity over metal oxides. However, their monotonic pore size distribution with indefinite structure cannot restrict the number of possible cycles [200]. Moreover, problems associated with stacking or agglomeration in MWCNTs are quite common [201].
To solve these pivotal problems, two main strategies have been explored to enhance the electrochemical performance of metal oxide-based anodes. This can be done by controlling their surface morphologies, coating conducting buffer layers (such as carbon, graphene, and conducting polymers (e.g., polypyrrole)), and making composites with carbonaceous materials [202,203]. However, some of the above approaches suffer from the drawback of complicated synthesis procedures, low specific surface area, and high costs. Similarly, pristine MOF-based anode materials suffer from insufficient rate capability and poor electrical conductivity. Thus, one of the viable solutions is to synthesize composites with favorable characteristics [14,204,205]. Recently, zeolitic imidazolate frameworks (ZIFs) have attracted considerable attention due to their fascinating properties and because of their modularly tunable structures assembled from transition-metal clusters [206]. Inspired by their diverse configurations, ZIFs have been demonstrated as useful precursors/templates for the synthesis of metal oxides with controlled morphologies or porous carbon nanostructures via thermal decomposition under controlled atmospheres [206–208]. Among metal oxides, $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ is considered to be one of the most promising candidates for LIBs due to its high theoretical capacity $(890\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ ), low cost, high corrosion resistance potential, and excellent catalytic properties [209–211]. However, pristine $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ anodes were observed to suffer from severe irreversible capacity loss with a low coulombic efficiency. This is due to the sudden volume expansion and particle aggregation of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ during charging/discharging [211]. Therefore, the composites of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ with nanocarbons, particularly N-doped nanocarbons (such as N-doped carbon nanotubes [212], N-doped carbon foam [213], and N-doped graphene [209]) can act as a buffer layer to restrict volume expansion and aggregation of nanoparticles. Composites of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ with nanocarbons exhibited highly favorable electrochemical performances as anode materials for LIBs [214,215]. In 2015, Hou and co-workers reported a novel $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N}$ -doped porous carbon hybrid with a dodecahedron structure through the facile pyrolysis of ZIF-67 nanoparticles (Fig. 4) [176]. $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N}$ -doped porous carbon hybrids were tested as anode materials and showed superior electrochemical performance, e.g., an excellent rate capability of $560\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{hg}^{-1}$ at $10\mathrm{Ag}_{\cdot}^{-1}$ The sample maintained an outstanding cycling stability with $91.7\%$ capacity even after 110 cycles. This dramatic improvement in electrochemical performance of $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/N-P C}$ hybrid in terms of initial cycle columbic efficiency and cyclability was attributed to its unique structural design [176].
In another report, a ZIF-67 derived $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ was synthesized and coated over nitrogen-doped carbon $(\mathsf{C o}_{3}0_{4}@\mathsf{N}-\mathsf{C})$ possessing a starfish like morphology [216]. The synthesized material was porous enough to buffer the volume expansion during charging/discharging cycles. It exhibited an excellent capacity of $785\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at a current density of $0.5\mathrm{mAg}^{-1}$ . When assembled with a ${\mathrm{LiFePO}}_{4}$ - based cathode, it remained stable up to 100 cycles with a capacity retention of about $95\%$ [216]. $\mathrm{Co}_{3}0_{4}/$ Nitrogen doped carbon has also reported with a specific capacity of $423\mathrm{\mAh\g^{-1}}$ after 100 cycles at the rate of $100\mathrm{mAg}^{-1}$ [217]. Similarly, $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{S}_{4}$ have been encapsulated by carbon layers, derived from ZIF-67. A composite of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{S}_{4}$ with $\mathsf{M o S}_{2}$ significantly improved the capacity and stability due to the involvement of layered structure [218]. $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{S}_{4}@.$ - $\mathsf{C@M o S}_{2}$ composite could retain a capacity of $672.6\mathrm{mAhg}^{-1}$ after 200 cycles which was much better than $\mathsf{C o}_{3}\mathsf{S}_{4}@\mathsf{M o S}_{2}$ whose capacity decreased to $20{-}25\mathrm{mAhg^{-1}}$ after 200 cycles [218]. The mechanical properties of MOFs were also improved by establishing a covalent link with benzoic acid functionalized graphene (BFG) [219]. Carboxylate groups in BFG can significantly enhance the metal-chelation strength between MOF and graphene, thus giving rise to a more compatible 3D hybrid framework. Therefore, one can conclude that doping and composite formation are considered to be the best strategies to improve the conductivity and rate capabilities [219]. In another report, a versatile layer by layer (LBL) assembly method was employed to synthesize $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}@\mathrm{HKUST}-1$ composites with a micro spherical core [220]. After 100 chargedischarge cycles at a current density of $100\mathrm{mAg}^{-1}$ , the reversible capacity of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}@\mathrm{MOF}$ composite electrode was observed to be ${\sim}1002\ \mathrm{mAh}\ \mathbf{g}^{-1}$ , which was much higher than that of the bare $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ counterpart $(696\mathrm{\mAh\g^{-1}};$ ). Moreover, the sample delivered a good reversible capacity of ${\sim}429\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ even at a very high current density of $2\mathsf{A}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ (the current density step increased from 0.1 to $2\mathsf{A}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ after 70 cycles) [220].
Composites of MOFs with carbon-based materials have also been synthesized to improve the electrical conductivity of insulated MOF structures through the exploitation of outstanding electrical properties of CNTs and graphene (possessing delocalized conducting electrons upon $\mathrm{Li}^{+}$ intercalation) [221,222]. Similarly, covalently reinforced $\mathrm{Co}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\mathrm{BDC}_{I}$ /carboxyl graphene (CGr) composites were synthesized with different concentrations of CGr to improve the rate capability and electrical conductivity of pristine MOFs (Fig. 3) [167]. The composites prepared with 5 wt% CGr concentration showed the highest initial discharge capacity of $2566\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and a reversible capacity of $1368\mathrm{mAhg^{-1}}$ at $100\mathrm{mAg}^{-1}$ , which remained stable up to $818~\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{h}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at $1\mathsf{A}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ after 400 repeated cycles. The improved capacity was attributed to the fact that $C0^{2+}$ (with three localized electrons) was converted into high-spin ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ (possessing delocalized electrons) upon $\mathrm{Li}^{+}$ intercalation, increasing the overall conductivity of the electrode. The most probable cause for the observed capacity decreases in the case of CGr $(10\mathrm{wt\%})$ was the loss of redox active material and poor transportation kinetics of the electrolyte (due to a small surface area) [167].
Along similar lines, a solvothermal treatment was employed to synthesize MIL-101(Cr)/graphene oxide composites via a facile route [181]. The results were compared with that for pristine MIL-101(Cr). Interestingly, the observed specific capacity of MIL101(Cr)/GO was almost two times larger than that of pristine MIL-101(Cr) at the first discharge process, and this increase was maintained over 40 cycles. The observed first discharge capacities of MIL-101(Cr) and MIL-101(Cr)/GO were $203.6\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and $445.7\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ (at a current density of $200\mathrm{mAg}^{-1},$ ), respectively. The improved electrochemical performance (in terms of rate capability and cycling stability) of MIL-101(Cr) can be attributed to its highly porous structure coupled with the presence of uniformly doped GO species in the framework [181]. The high conductivity of graphene also enhances the electrode performance by greatly reducing the diffusion time, accelerating the phase transfer reaction, and enhancing the overall conductivity. Moreover, these graphene composites were advantageous enough to efficiently prevent aggregation of electrode materials and volume expansion/contraction during $\mathrm{Li}^{+}$ intercalation/deintercalation.
Similarly, a combination of reduced graphene oxide (rGO) and Fe-MOF was achieved through the synthesis of Fe-MOF/reduced graphene oxide (rGO) composites via a solvothermal method [223]. In the Fe-MOF structure, $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ is an important lithium storage site due to its redox activity (conversion from $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ to ones with lower oxidation status such as $\mathsf{F e}^{2+}$ , $\mathsf{F e}^{+}$ , and $\mathsf{F e}^{0}$ ) [224–227]. In addition, the carboxylic groups of terephthalic acid ligands in FeMOF can also act as electrochemically active sites. However, these sites are usually inactive due to poor conductivity and thick SEI formation on the electrode surface during the initial charging/discharging [223]. This downside of MOFs can be removed by combining them with conductive agents such as carbon species or conductive polymers [228–231].
rGO is considered to be a promising material to use with MOFs due to its unique capability to resist the formation of a SEI layer as well as its superior conductivity [232–234]. In view of this, Jin et al. (2016) prepared three different samples in which the percentage of rGO was altered to optimize the composite configurations [235]. These authors demonstrated the effect of rGO amount in the FeMOF structure in relation to the overall cell capacity. In their study, the Fe-MOF/rGO $(5\%)$ composite showed the best Li storage with a maximum reversible capacity of $2259\mathrm{mAhg}^{-1}$ in the first cycle (which became stable up to $1010\mathrm{mAhg}^{-1}$ after 200 cycles) at a current density of $500\mathrm{mAg}^{-1}$ (Fig. 6).
The first discharge/charge capacities of Fe-MOF/rGO ( $0\%$ , $5\%$ , and $10\%$ ) composites were 2213/786, 2259/891.1, and $1890/750\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively; these results also corresponded to initial coulombic efficiencies of $35.5\%$ , $43.3\%$ , and $39.7\%$ , respectively [235]. Similarly, the MIL-53(Fe)/rGO composite was synthesized to give a reversible capacity of $550\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{hg}^{-1}$ at a current rate of $100\mathrm{mAg}^{-1}$ after 100 charge/discharge cycles [223]. For comparison, the electrochemical performance of pristine MIL-53 was just $310\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{hg}^{-1}$ at a current density of $100\mathrm{mAg}^{-1}$ The non-favorable result of MIL-53 was due to the low conductivity of MIL-53. In addition to this, the close contact of porous MIL-53 with electrolyte resulted in the formation of a thick SEI layer. This thick electrolyte layer suppressed the electrochemical activity of carboxylic group. In contrast, the hydrophilic properties of rGO allowed the electrolyte to directly contact the surface of the electrode material when rGO was introduced into the composite [223]. Apart from rGO, Jin and co-workers have recently synthesized a 2D NiCo-MOF in combination with black phosphorus (BP) [237]. BP attracted an attention due to its thermodynamic stability and insolubility in most solvents [238]. Moreover, the layered structure of BP allowed its exfoliation into few layers through mechanical or liquid phase exfoliation [239]. On combining BP with NiCo-MOF, these samples delivered a specific capacity of $853\mathrm{\mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at the rate of $0.5\mathsf{A g}^{-1}$ . After the 1000 charging/discharging cycles, it maintained a specific capacity of $398\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at the rate of $5\mathsf{A}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ [237].
Several issues must be resolved before metal oxides $(\mathsf{M O}_{\mathrm{x}},$ $\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}$ , Fe, Ni, Co, Sn etc.) in LIBs can be practically used. The lithium storage mechanism based on the conversion reaction suffers from large volume change upon lithiation/delithiation, and this can result in severe electrode pulverization [240]. The electrode pulverization led to the failure of electrical contacts between the electrode and current collector, which eventually resulted in capacity fading. Secondly, transition metal oxides usually suffer from poor electrical conductivity, which renders them disadvantageous as high-power density electrodes [241]. Additionally, the formation of a stable SEI layer on transition metal oxides is not possible due to the limited cyclability of the LIBs, which is due to the repeated volume expansion and shrinkage of electrode materials [242,243].
Several methods have been identified for accommodating the volume expansion and enhancing the electrical conductivities of the electrodes. For instance, successful nanostructure engineering of transition metal oxide materials has been demonstrated in LIBs to mitigate volume change-induced mechanical strain [121–124,244–247]. Therefore, hybridization with carbonaceous materials (e.g., graphene, graphene oxides, carbon nanotubes, and conductive polymers) has been proposed to enhance the conductivity of transition metal oxide electrodes [248–252]. MOFs were also used recently to prepare advanced transition metal oxide electrodes due to their ability to form a well-organized nanostructure and to intercalate with other materials (like graphene, graphene oxides, carbon nanotubes, and conductive polymers). Likewise, nickel-based MOFs (Ni-MOFs) were synthesized with hollow ZIF8 ball-in-ball structures via a solvo-thermal approach (Fig. 4) [177]. The as-synthesized MOFs were further utilized in the preparation of NiO/Ni/graphene composites as anode materials for LIBs. The composite exhibited a highly reversible capacity of $942\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{h}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ in the first charge/discharge cycle with a gradual increase to $1180\mathrm{mAhg}^{-1}$ after 250 charge/discharge cycles. The capacity of the electrode decreased from $1127\mathrm{mAhg^{-1}}$ to $1030\mathrm{mAhg^{-1}}$ when the current density increased from $200\mathrm{mAg}^{-1}$ to $8000\mathrm{mAg}^{-1}$ In addition, the graphene coating enhanced the electrode conductivity while providing a buffer layer for mitigating the volume expansion. This further promoted the formation of a stable SEI, which was the primary reason the reversible capacity reached $962\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{h}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ after 1000 cycles. This exceptional electrochemical performance was attributed to the fast $\mathrm{Li}^{+}$ transport, easy electron mobility, and enhanced conductivity due to the unique structure of graphene coated Ni-MOFs [177].
Along with Ni-based oxides, $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ also possesses a very high theoretical capacity of $1006\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . However, due to the slow transport/diffusion of ${\mathrm{Li}}^{+}$ ions and very fast capacity attenuation, the practical application of $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ in LIBs is severely limited [245,253]. Many researchers have focused on the fabrication of $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ -based nanostructures to promote ion diffusion and electron transfer while effectively reducing the large volume changes (occurring during lithiation/delithiation) to prevent electrode disintegration [254–257]. Recently, $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ -based composites (e.g., $\mathrm{Fe_{2}O_{3}/M n O_{2}}$ [258], $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/F e_{2}O_{3}}$ [259], and $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}0_{3}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ [260]) have been studied, and these exhibited high capacity and rate capabilities that were far superior to the pristine $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ . A porous $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}0_{3}/$ $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ composite was also prepared from the $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}/Z n-M O F}$ and examined as an anode material for LIBs [261]. Accordingly, the composite anode delivered a high theoretical lithium storage capacity $(978\mathrm{\mAh\g^{-1}}.$ ) for $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ relative to traditional graphitic anode materials. The composites exhibited excellent electrochemical performance, including good charge/discharge specific capacity with increased rate capacity and a relatively stable cycle performance (reversible capacity of $700\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at a current density of $100\mathrm{mAg}^{-1}$ after 60 cycles). These authors reported initial discharge/charge capacities as high as 1344 and $1189\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively, for $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}0_{3}/\mathrm{ZnO}$ composites derived from MOFs, indicating a low irreversible capacity loss of ${\sim}13\%$ . After 60 cycles, the discharge capacity remained at $700\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ for $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}0_{3}/\mathrm{ZnO}$ whereas it was only $390\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ for the pristine $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ . The enhanced performance of $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}0_{3}/\mathrm{ZnO}$ was attributed to the formation of a litchi-shaped product, which can accommodate the strain derived from lithium insertion/extrusion [261].
Along similar lines, $\mathsf{C o F e}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{4}$ , a binary metal oxide derived from a bimetallic organic framework (BOF) was synthesized and wrapped with graphene and subsequently tested as an anode for LIBs [262]. The anode yielded an excellent discharge capacity of $1062\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ after 100 charge/discharge cycles at a current density of $100\mathrm{mAg}^{-1}$ . Also, it retained a specific capacity of $75.1\%$ which is much higher than the previously reported Co oxide/GNS and Fe oxide/GN-based composite electrodes [263–267]. In comparison, pristine ${\mathsf{C o F e}}_{2}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}$ delivered a discharge capacity of $532.7\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{hg}^{-1}$ with a capacity retention of $53.8\%$ [262]. The same strategy was followed by Li et al. (2019) who have synthesized a mixed metal MOF (Co-Zn) composite with carbon fiber (CF) [268]. This MOF composite was further annealed to obtain $\boldsymbol{Z}\boldsymbol{\mathrm{nCo}}_{2}.$ - $_{\mathrm{0_{4}/C F}}$ composite which has been explored as an anode material for LIBs. The $\mathrm{ZnCo_{2}O_{4}/C F}$ composite delivered a specific capacity of $463\mathrm{\mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at the rate of $50\mathrm{mAg^{-1}}$ for 100 cycles of charging/ discharging [268]. Li and co-workers also synthesized $Z_{\mathrm{nO}/\mathrm{NiO}}$ microspheres with a nanorod-composed shell and a microsphere yolk $2\upmu\mathrm{m}$ diameter) through a controlled calcination treatment of BOF [269]. The synthesized uniform microspheres showed an excellent specific capacity of $1008.6\mathrm{mAhg^{-1}}$ at a current density of $0.1\mathrm{Ag}^{-1}$ after 200 cycles. Also, the capacity of $437.1\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{h}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at a high current density of $2\mathsf{A}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ was retained to support their superior rate capability [269]. Similarly, a scalable method of fabricating integrated $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/T i O_{2}}$ composite hollow polyhedrons was proposed through a cation-exchange approach using MOFs (Fig. 4) [178]. Using a thermal treatment, these authors synthesized well-defined ZIF-67 particles, which acted as a template to form hollow polyhedrons and simultaneously serve as a host to accommodate exchanged titanium cations. In this structure, $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ provided a stable backbone resulting in a high capacity in $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ and served as a conductive path for transportation of electrons. The obtained integrated $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/T i O_{2}}$ composite hollow polyhedrons provided a very high initial capacity of $662\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{h}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ with a stable reversible capacity of $642\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{h}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at a current density of $500\mathrm{mAg}^{-1}$ after 200 cycles. As such, the ending values corresponded to $96.9\%$ of the initial capacity (much higher than that of pristine $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ with a specific capacity of $200\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and a capacity retention of $40\%$ ) [178].
Composites of hybridized nanostructured metal oxides with MWCNTs are highly desirable due to their potential to improve the electrochemical performance of LIBs. In this respect, MWCNTs $\scriptstyle{\left\vert\mathbf{C}\mathbf{0}_{3}\mathbf{O}_{4}\right.}$ nanocomposites were proposed in which a morphology-maintained annealing treatment was applied for carbon nanotubes inserted in MOFs [270]. The designed MWCNT/ $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ combined the high theoretical capacity and excellent conductivity of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ while maintaining the strong mechanical/chemical stability of MWCNTs. This nanocomposite, when tested as anode materials for LIBs, displayed a high reversible capacity of $813\mathrm{\mAh\g^{-1}}$ at a current density of $100\mathrm{mAg}^{-1}$ after 100 charge/discharge cycles. Even at a current density of $1000\mathrm{mAg}^{-1}$ , a stable specific capacity of $514\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{h}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ could be maintained. The observed merits of $\mathsf{M W C N T/C o_{3}O_{4}}$ (e.g., improved reversible capacity, excellent cycling stability, and good rate capability) can be attributed to the hierarchical porous structure and the synergistic effect between $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ and MWCNTs [270].
## 4. MOFs and their composites as cathode materials for Li-S batteries
Herbert and Ulam first suggested the use of sulfur as a positive electrode in metal-sulfur batteries in 1960 (Fig. 7) [271]. To date, many other types of batteries have been developed including lithium iron phosphate $\left(\mathrm{LiFePO}_{4}\right)$ (high specific capacity), lithium cobalt oxide $\begin{array}{r}{\left(\operatorname{LiCoO}_{2}\right)}\end{array}$ ) (highly stable and most adopted), lithium polymer (LiPo) (containing polymer electrolyte), and lithium titanate (LTO) (known for its fast charging) [272–274]. However, all the available LIBs suffer from serious drawbacks. For example, $\mathsf{L i F e P O}_{4}$ exhibits low energy density, low electrical conductivity, and low operating potential. $\operatorname{LiCoO}_{2}$ and LiPo have limited capacity, whereas LTO has a low terminal voltage [272,274]. Hence, Li-S batteries are strongly considered to be the best substitute over other Li-based batteries. This is due to their high energy density, which can be obtained from the high theoretical capacity of sulfur $\mathrm{'}1675\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{h}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\mathrm{\Omega},$ [17]. The second major factor for the popularity of Li-S batteries is the cost effectiveness of sulfur (approximately priced at $\$100$ ton) [275]. Li (the lightest metal) and sulfur (moderate weight) are considered to be excellent materials to fabricate light and powerful batteries [276]. Presently, Li-S batteries can give a much higher capacity of $500\mathrm{mAhg}^{-1}$ as compared to the Li-ion ( $:150{-}200\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1};$ [277]. If temperature is a concern, Li-S batteries can work at much lower temperatures as compared to LIBs. This is the main reason why these batteries were used in 2008 in solar powered airplanes to attain the maximum possible altitude [278]. Moreover, Li-S batteries are eco-friendly and can be disposed of in the environment without any kind of risk to wildlife, plants, and living beings [17,276,279–281].
The electrochemistry of Li-S batteries involves large amounts of polysulfide products [286,287]. The overall reaction during discharging of cells is as follows:
$$
S_{8}+2\ensuremath{\mathrm{e^{-}}}\rightarrow S_{8}^{2-}
$$
$$
S_{8}^{2-}+S_{6}^{2-}\to1/4S_{8}
$$
$$
2S_{6}^{2-}+2e^{-}\rightarrow3S_{4}^{2-}
$$
$$
3S_{4}^{2-}+2{\mathrm e}^{-}\rightarrow4S_{3}^{2-}
$$
$$
2\mathrm{S}_{3}^{2-}+6\mathrm{Li}^{+}+2\mathrm{e}^{-}\rightarrow3\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{2}
$$
$$
\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{2}+2\mathrm{Li}^{+}+2\mathbf{e}^{-}\rightarrow2\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}
$$
During the charging phase, the above reaction proceeds in the reverse direction. The high theoretical specific capacity of Li-S batteries is associated with the formation of $\mathrm{Li}_{2}S$ as the final product. $\mathrm{Li}_{2}S$ contains two $\mathrm{Li}^{+}$ ions and one sulfur atom, whereas the conventional LIBs hold only $0.7\mathrm{-}0.8\mathrm{Li}^{+}$ ions over a single atom. However, the Li-S batteries can suffer from many shortcomings in a practical sense that limit their real-world applications [288]. The serious drawbacks of Li-S batteries include: (i) the solubility of higher order polysulfide in the organic electrolyte, which leads to faded capacity (attributed to the loss of active material) because after dissolving they are not easy to extract back, (ii) the insulating nature of sulfur and $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathsf{S}_{\mathrm{X}}$ increases the internal resistance of the battery and fading of the coulombic efficiency, and (iii) the shuttle effect of polysulfide (the motion of polysulfide product towards the lithium metal and sulfur) [55]. This shuttle effect arises due to the soluble nature of higher polysulfides $(\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{8}$ and $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{6}$ ), which tend to move towards lithium metal. On reacting with lithium metal, they are converted into lower polysulfides ( $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathsf S_{4}$ and $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{3}$ ), which further move towards the sulfur electrode to form higher polysulfides $\mathrm{\Li_{2}S_{8}}$ and $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{6}.$ ). Thus, the shuttling effect of the discharge products can cause potential failures in Li-S batteries [289–291].
## 4.1. MOFs and their derived structures as cathode materials for Li-S batteries
MOFs with high porosity and favorable distribution of micro/ meso pores can be used to effectively store chain-type structures of sulfur prior to discharging, while effectively trapping polysulfides upon discharge [292]. Moreover, the functional groups (e.g., oxygen, nitrogen, and boron) present in MOFs can form bonds with lithium polysulfides to upgrade the performance and cycling stability of Li-S batteries [293]. The surface area of the MOFs also helps to achieve the maximum possible specific capacity in Li-S batteries. The surface area of the MOF determines the loading of sulfur (which is an active material in Li-S batteries). Therefore, a high surface area electrode material indirectly indicates the specific capacity of the battery.
The first usage of MOFs as electrode materials for a Li-S battery was reported by D-Cakan et al. [294], who used a mesoporous chromium-based MOF (MIL-100(Cr)) as a host material for sulfur impregnation. The successful impregnation of $48\mathrm{wt\%}$ sulfur into the pores of MIL-100 (with a surface area of $1485\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}.$ was done at a temperature of $155^{\circ}\mathsf C$ . The surface area of the MIL- ${\cdot}100(\mathsf{C r})@S$ was $360\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ after successful impregnation of sulfur in the MOF matrix. This impregnation of sulfur showed a remarkable increase in the overall specific capacity of the above matrix. The asprepared MIL- $100(\mathrm{Cr})@S$ samples with $50\%$ carbon gave a first cycle capacity of $1150{-}1170\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{hg}^{-1}$ , which decreased to about $500\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ after 59 cycles at a current density of $0.1\mathsf C$ However, the XPS (X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy) data revealed the migration of polysulfide into the electrolyte, which is a direct indication of the weak binding between the oxygenated MOF groups and polysulfide [294].
Continuing research efforts were mainly directed toward sulfur encapsulation and interactions between the polysulfide and MOF structures. In 2013, Wang et al. used HKUST-1 MOF by making a composite with $40\%$ wt. sulfur, which was then heated to $428\mathrm{K}$ for $24\mathrm{h}$ [295]. These authors compared the electrochemical performances of HKUST- $1@S$ at room temperature. The two forms of MOFs, HKUST- $1@S$ (428 K) and HKUST- $1@S$ (room temperature), exhibited excellent specific capacities of $1498\mathrm{mAhg^{-1}}$ and $1507\mathrm{mAhg}^{-1}$ , respectively. However, the specific capacity of the latter faded very rapidly to $350\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ just after 50 cycles of charging/discharging. In contrast, the former was stabilized at $500\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ after 170 cycles at a current density of $0.1\mathsf C.$ . The encapsulation of sulfur in MOF structures was confirmed by the fact that the BET surface area of the composite decreased from 1500 to $97\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . The enhanced stability of the structure of HKUST- $\cdot1@{\cal S}$ (428 K) after 70 cycles of charging/discharging was also confirmed through the XRD analysis (Fig. 8) [295]. Furthermore, there is a pressing need to study the interactions between polysulfides and metallic clusters [296]. In this regard, the prevalent interactions were correlated based on a Lewis acid base concept [293]. These authors synthesized Ni-MOF $(\mathsf{N i}_{6}(\mathsf{B T B})_{4}(\mathsf{B P})_{3}$ ( ${\mathrm{BTB}}=$ benzene-1,3,5-tribenzoate and $\mathsf{B P}=4,4^{\prime}-$ bipyridyl)) in which $\mathsf{N i}(\mathrm{II})$ acted as a Lewis acid, while polysulfide served as a Lewis base. The prepared sample showed a reversible capacity of $689\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at a current density of $0.1\mathsf C$ . This interaction is associated with the fact that the specific capacity of the battery remains stable at $89\%$ even after 100 cycles, most probably due to the inhibition of polysulfide dissolution in the electrolyte. These authors also demonstrated a specific order of stable complexes (Mn (II) < Fe (II) $<\mathsf{C o}$ (II) ${<\mathsf{N i}}$ $(\mathrm{II})<C\mathrm{u}$ (II)) that can act as strong Lewis acids; such an observation is in good agreement with the Irving-Williams series, which also follows the same method of selecting a MOF as a host for sulfur [297]. Moreover, the battery efficiency can be improved by considering the effect of organic linkages and optimizing the $S/{\mathsf{M O F}}/C$ ratio [293]. Similarly, Liu et al. demonstrated a manganese cluster based Mn-MOF that can provide sufficient chemical adsorption and physical capture of polysulfide [298]. Moreover, Mn-MOFs equipped with cubic cages can provide sufficiently high impregnation of sulfur. Activated sites provided by the nine open metal sites in Mn-MOF sufficiently inhibited the polysulfide loss. The Mn-MOF showed a specific capacity of $990\mathrm{mAh}\ \mathbf{g}^{-1}$ after 200 cycles of charging/discharging when used as a cathode in Li-S batteries [298].
The effect of particle size was investigated in relation to the specific capacity and the cycling performance [299]. These authors prepared five samples of ZIF-8 with different particle sizes and observed that the utilization of sulfur in ZIF-8 increased as particle size decreased. ZIF-8 had the advantage of an aperture with an effective diameter of ${\sim}11.6\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ and a hexagonal aperture of about ${\sim}3.4\mathring{\mathsf{A}}.$ , which is sufficient to store the chain like structure of the sulfur (Fig. $\mathsf{g a-c}$ ) [300]. According to the TGA and XRD analyses, the structure was not distorted by up to $30\%$ sulfur loading. However, the particle size directly affected the battery performance due to its influence on the transportation of polysulfides within the grains of the electrode material and leaching of polysulfides in electrolyte. These phenomena can be controlled based on the particle size and can in turn influence the utilization and retention of sulfur. Therefore, the selection of a suitable particle size for the host material was crucial in determining the performance of the battery. The sample with a particle size $<20\mathrm{nm}$ gave the highest capacity of $950\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at a current density of $0.5\mathsf{C}$ . However, the best cycle efficiency was achieved with moderate particle sizes of ${\sim}200\mathrm{nm}$ $75\%$ over 250 cycles at 0.5 C) [300]. In general, smaller particle sizes helped achieve a high capacity, while a smaller aperture with easily accessible functionalities in the open framework helped stabilize the cyclability of Li-S batteries [301].
In short, more research is needed to stabilize the cycling performance and capacity of the Li-S batteries. Particle size, porosity, and the particle aperture are the main factors that govern the storability of sulfur and control the dissolution of lithium polysulfide into the electrolyte. More research should focus on the effect of different metallic centers in the MOFs. Note that the metallic center plays a major role in controlling the interaction of MOFs with polysulfide. Thus, the nature of the interactions can greatly inhibit/catalyze the migration of the polysulfide into the electrolyte [293,302]. Additionally, organic ligands containing oxygen functionalities can also be considered for polysulfides. Basically, the oxygen group interacts to form bonds with metallic clusters that are conducting in nature to yield improved electrochemical performance of the battery [303].
Apart from the research discussed above, S@MOFs still face some critical issues (like very low conductivity and the addition of non-active material (host matrix, binder, and conducting agent)) that result in the loss of specific capacity in the battery. The addition of conductive agents in the MOF-based cathode increased the conductivity of the material. However, this impeded the high loading of sulfur content in the cathode and decreased the amount of available active centers. Therefore, MOF derivatives can be used as an alternative material to resolve or minimize these issues. One benefit of the MOF derived material is the adoption of a unique morphology, ultrahigh surface area, micro-meso pores, and fitted pore size ranges from the parent (Fig. 5). In view of this observation, a carbonaceous material with hierarchical pores was prepared through the pyrolysis of different MOFs [236]. These authors selected four different MOFs to derive hierarchical porous carbon species including (ZIF-8) [304], room temperature MOF-5 [305], solvothermal synthesized MOF-5 (solvo-MOF-5) [306], and $\mathrm{\small{[Zn_{3}(f u m a r a t e)_{3}(d m f)_{2}]}}$ (Zn Fumarate) [307]. The presence of mesopores in the material was responsible for the higher initial capacity, whereas the micropores were responsible for achieving stable cyclic behavior [308–311]. Out of the four carbons, the one derived from Zn-fumarate exhibited the highest pore volume of $3.99\mathrm{cm}^{3}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ along with excellent mesopores $(2.24\thinspace{\mathsf{c m}}^{3}\thinspace{\mathbf{g}}^{-1}),$ . Electrochemical performance tests of the carbons showed a maximum initial capacity of $1472\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , which was about $88\%$ of the theoretical capacity of Li-S batteries. Room temperature synthesized MOF-5 exhibited the maximum amount of mesopores with negligible macropores and achieved a capacity of $592.1\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{h}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ over 40 cycles (the best cyclability among all the tested materials). The other samples (ZIF-8 and solvo-thermal MOF-5) showed poor capacity due to the presence of a large number of macropores (Fig. 6).
On similar lines, hierarchically porous carbon nanoplates (HPCN) were synthesized from MOF-5 with a well-defined 3D porous plate nanostructure with a high surface area of $1645\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and a large pore volume of $1.18\mathrm{cm}^{3}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ [312]. This nanostructure was used to encapsulate the cyclic octa atomic structure of sulfur, which may play a vital role as a strong absorbent to suppress the dissolution of polysulfide and limit the volume change of the cathode during the interconversion reaction. The prepared sample (HPCN-S) exhibited a high initial specific capacity of 1177 mA h $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ at a current density of 0.1 C. Even at a high current density of $0.5\mathsf{C}$ , it delivered a reversible specific capacity of $730\mathrm{mAhg}^{-1}$ over 50 cycles [312]. In another report, a micro-porous carbon polyhedron (MPCP) was also prepared from a unique polyhedral morphological MOF (ZIF-8) [313]. The MPCP was loaded with sulfur at concentrations of $43\%$ (MPCP-I) and $63\%$ (MPCP-II). These samples were tested in different electrolyte conditions (e.g., 1 M $\mathrm{LiPF}_{6}$ in $\mathsf{E C}/$ DEC, 1 M LiTFSI in TEGDME, and 1 M LiTFSI in DOL/DME). However, the obtained results were unsatisfactory with the highest capacity obtained with MPCP-1 being only $490\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ for 50 cycles of charging/discharging [313]. The results of this study suggest further research for cathodic materials through the optimization of several components (e.g., the electrolyte, sulfur content, and meso/micro-pores) to efficiently encapsulate sulfur and to obtain good electrolyte penetration.
In view of the above reports, a carbonaceous material was derived through the pyrolysis of ZIF-8 and was subsequently used to wrap a graphene sheet over the $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{C@S}}$ composite via an ultrasonic method [180]. This strategy helped achieve excellent electrical conductivity as well as mechanical flexibility, which can improve electron transport and accommodate the volume change during charging/discharging cycles. Additionally, this approach offered a tunable porous structured carbon in which electro-active sulfur can be easily diffused. The prepared sample delivered an initial discharge capacity of $1171\ \mathrm{mAhg}^{-1}$ , which became stable at $561\mathrm{\mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ (current density of $168\mathrm{\mAh{\bfg}}^{-1};$ . The capacity retention of the tested electrode was $48\%$ with an excellent coulombic efficiency of $90\%$ [180]. Likewise, a novel French frylike hierarchical porous carbon (FLHPC) was obtained with a onedimensional (1D) structure through the pyrolysis of Al-MOF (to suppress the shuttle effect in Li-S battery) [314]. These samples recorded a capacity of $1100\mathrm{mAhg^{-1}}$ at a current density of $0.5{\sf C}_{\mathrm{\scriptscriptstyle\cdot}}$ , which remained stable with a capacity retention of $68\%$ . A capacity as high as $763\mathrm{mAhg^{-1}}$ was obtained at a high current density of $2\mathsf{C}$ after 20 cycles [314]. In another literature, porous carbon sponges (MOF-C) were derived through the pyrolysis of Al-MOF $\ @800^{\circ}\mathsf C$ under Ar flow [315]. These MOF-C sponges were further incorporated with $\mathsf{A l}_{2}(0\mathsf{H})_{2.76}\mathsf{F}_{3.24}$ hydroxy halide nanoparticles (having polar bonds) MOF- $\mathsf{C}/\mathsf{A l}_{2}(\mathsf{O H})_{2.76}\mathsf{F}_{3.24}$ . In addition to this work, MOF- $\mathsf{C}/\mathsf{A l}_{2}(0\mathsf{H})_{2.76}\mathsf{F}_{3.24}$ was modified by twining CNTs through the electrostatic interactions of positively charged MOFs-C and negatively charged CNTs. CNTs with a MOF-C interconnected 3D structure provided an effective pathway for $\mathrm{Li}^{+}$ and $\mathrm{e}^{-}$ transport while charging/discharging processes resulted in enhanced charge transfer kinetics. The prepared CNTs/MOF- $.{\mathsf{C}}/{\mathsf{\Omega}}$ $\mathrm{Al_{2}(O H)_{2.76}F_{3.24}/S}$ cathode delivered a specific capacity of $889\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at a current density of $500\mathrm{mAg}^{-1}$ over 300 cycles. Moreover, a specific capacity of $719\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ was obtained at a very high current density of 2 C $1\mathrm{C}=1680\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{hg}^{-1}.$ ) with a coulombic efficiency of approximately $100\%$ [315].
It is vital to address the issue of shuttle effect in Li-S batteries to promote their commercial success. This can be done with the use of hybrid materials. In this context, ZIF-67 has been utilized to derive a highly porous nitrogen-carbon-cobalt (N-C-Co) interlayer that was proven to be useful to inhibit the polysulfide losses in the electrolyte [317]. The above layer not only suppressed the migration of $\operatorname{Li}_{2}\mathsf{S}_{\mathsf{X}}$ to the Li-metal anode in the presence of organic electrolyte but also efficiently absorbed (both physically and chemically) the free polysulfides. Under laboratory conditions, the above device yielded an initial discharge capacity of $1216.9\mathrm{mAhg^{-1}}$ at a rate of 1 C. This value decreased to $660.3\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{h}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ after 250 cycles at a sulfur loading of $1.5\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ . Importantly, the system delivered a coulombic efficiency of $99\%$ , suggesting a successful suppression of polysulfide loss in electrolyte [317].
The use of ZIF-8 was proposed as a precursor for the synthesis of oxygenated nitrogen doped microporous carbon (OMNC) (Fig. 10) [316]. Since it is desirable to restrict any polysulfide product toward the cathodic side of a Li-S battery, this OMNC material was coated over the separator side facing the CNT/S cathode. OMNC not only efficiently interacted with $\mathrm{Li^{+}}$ ions due to the rich presence of carboxyl and hydroxyl groups, it also effectively suppressed the polysulfide shuttle effect owing to its nitrogen doping. Recently, the synthesis of Co doped carbon was proposed through the pyrolysis of Co-MOF that was in-situ pre-grown over clews of polymer nanobelts (CsPNBs) [318]. The above prepared product, when used as a cathode electrode, yielded an initial capacity of $1300\mathrm{mAhg}^{-1}$ , which became stable to $400\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ after 610 cycles of charging/discharging at a rate of $0.5{\sf C}$ [318]. The pyrolytic conversion of ZIF-67 in to nanoporous carbon was also reported [318]. The application of this nanoporous carbon as a cathode in a Li-S battery could deliver a specific capacity value of $781.1\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{h}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at the current density of $0.5{\sf C}$ for 140 cycles of charging/discharging. This outstanding capacity of ZIF-67 derived nanoporous carbon was attributed to the presence of a high sulfur content $(50\mathrm{wt\%})$ in the electrode material [318].
## 4.2. MOF composites as cathode materials for Li-S batteries
The low conductivity of MOFs is a crucial factor which prevents them from achieving high electrochemical performance. If the electrode material does not possess enough electronic conductivity, the battery would deliver a small discharge capacity while experiencing power losses. Hence, to reduce this critical problem and to completely use the stable structure of MOFs, researchers have explored MOF composites with conductive materials (e.g., conductive polymers and carbon). The use of MOF composites for Li-S batteries was first reported by Zhou et al. [181], who prepared mesoporous MIL-101 $({\mathrm{Cr}}){\ @}{\Gamma}G0$ as a host cathodic electrode to improve the electrical conductivity of the MOF. Compared to pristine MOF-101 $(\mathbf{Cr})@S$ (discharge capacity of $458\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and $37.3\%$ capacity retention), MIL-101(Cr)@rGO delivered an initial discharge capacity of $980\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{h}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at a current density of $335\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , which became stable at $650\mathrm{{mA}}$ h $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ after 50 cycles [181]. These authors wrapped a graphene sheet over MIL-101(Cr)/sulfur composites (Fig. 11) and recorded an initial discharge capacity of $1190\mathrm{mAhg}^{-1}$ at a current density of $0.1\mathsf C,$ , which decreased to $19.9\%$ after 300 cycles. The coulombic efficiency remained up to $97\%$ even at a very high current density of $\ge1.6\mathsf{C}$ [181].
In another report, a composite material was prepared by interpenetrating CNTs in three different types of MOF (HKUST-1, ZIF-8, and MOF-5) crystals [319]. CNTs were interweaved within the electrode into a stratified structure to enhance the electrical conductivity and structural integrity. In contrast, the highly porous structure of MOFs provided the space to confine the chain like structure of sulfur. Out of all the tested samples, HKUST-1 gave the best results. The author mixed the CNTs and HKUST-1 in a 2:3 ratio with $40\%$ wt. sulfur. The tested sample delivered an initial discharge capacity of $1263\mathrm{mAhg^{-1}}$ at a current density of $0.2\mathsf{C}.$ . Even at high current densities of 2 C and $10{\mathsf{C}}$ , the sample delivered good capacities of 880 and $449\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{h}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively. The capacity recovered to $1102\mathrm{mAhg^{-1}}$ when the current density went back to $_{0.2\mathsf{C}}$ [319]. On similar lines, Jiang and co-workers have described the MOF derived oxide with polypyrrole (ppy) for the high charge/discharge kinetics [320]. The porous nature of MOF combined well with the conductive feature of ppy. Different MOFs (e.g., MIL101, MIL-53, and PCN-224) were combined with ppy, and their conductivity and electrochemical performance were found drastically increased. Due to fast charge transfer and diffusion kinetics, MIL-53/ppy exhibited a specific capacity of $1420\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at the rate of $0.1\mathrm{Ag}^{-1}$ . Likewise, PCN-224/ppy retained a specific capacity of $680\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ [320].
ZIF-67/graphene composites were synthesized with different concentrations of GO to investigate their electroactivity for Li-S cells [321]. One advantage of the ZIF-67 framework is the chemical interactions of Co with lithium polysulfide, which can make them immobile. These authors prepared four samples in which the GO concentration varied between 1 and $4\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ . Among the prepared samples, the $3\mathrm{mgL}^{-1}$ concentrated GO-ZIF-67 showed a maximum initial specific capacity of $1459\mathrm{mAhg^{-1}}$ at a rate of $100\mathrm{mAg}^{-1}$ . As the electrochemical test was done at a high current density of $1\mathsf{A}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ , the sample delivered a discharge capacity of $409\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . This capacity then returned to $739\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ as the current density decreased back to $100\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . However, the relationship between graphene concentration and the delivered capacity was not thoroughly investigated [321]. Similarly, ZIF-67 derived $\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}$ anchored with 3D graphene foam (3DGF) was prepared to achieve a high sulfur loading [322]. Moreover, the free standing $\mathrm{Co}{_{9}}\mathrm{S}_{8}/3\mathrm{D}\mathrm{GF}$ composite can be used as a cathode electrode without any further addition of binder, current collector, or conductive agent. At a sulfur loading of $10.4\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , the $\mathsf{C o}_{9}\mathsf{S}_{8}/3\mathsf{D}\mathsf{G F}$ composite achieved a specific capacity of $1300\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at a current density of $\mathsf C/20$ . The specific capacitance remained stable to $736\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ for 500 cycles of charging/discharging when the current density increased to 1 C [322].
Recently, sulfur loaded $\mathsf{F e}_{1-\mathbf{x}}S/$ nitrogen doped carbon composite was prepared through the sulfurization of Fe-MOF at $500^{\circ}\mathsf C$ [323]. When tested in Li-S batteries, the obtained sample exhibited a reversible specific capacity of $586\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at a higher current density of $5\mathsf{A}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ for 200 cycles of charging/discharging [323]. Apart from this, composites of a MOF with a highly flexible template can provide high porosity and mechanical flexibility to the overall sample [324]. Cellulose is well known for its non-toxic nature, biodegradability, inherent renewability, low cost, and low density [179]. Super-tough cellulose can act as a supporting host or scaffolding template to impregnate sulfur or other guest molecules [325–327]. Likewise, the same idea was implemented to store sulfur in bacterial cellulose (BC) as a guest molecule [328]. These authors used BC as a template due to the presence of abundant hydroxyl group terminals, which can offer reaction sites for the nucleation and growth of MOFs. In addition, BC can suppress the aggregation of individual MOF nanoparticles. They synthesized BC@UiO-66 aerogel film composites to store the sulfur [328]. The use of BC@UiO-66 as a cathode electrode yielded a specific capacity of $631\ \mathrm{mAh}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at a current density of $0.5\mathsf{C}$ over 100 cycles of charging/discharging. The sample BC@UiO-66 was further coated with polydopamine to prevent the non-conducting material from moving into the electrolyte. When used as a cathode, BC@UiO$66@\mathsf{P D A}$ showed a specific capacity of $739\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at a current density of $0.5{\sf C}$ over 100 cycles of charging/discharging [328]. Cheng et al. have reported the synthesis of ${\mathsf{N i}}_{2}{\mathsf{P}}@C$ composite through the phosphorization of MOF-74 (Ni) [329]. Further, a sample of sulfur ${\cdot\textcircled{a}}\mathrm{Ni}_{2}\mathrm{P}@C$ was suggested to exhibited a specific capacity of $1357\mathrm{\mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ even after 300 cycles [329]. Besides this, it is expected that interaction between oxides and polysulfides has been exploited to improve the performance of a $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}/\mathrm{C}$ composite. Note that this composite was derived from a Fe-MOF [330]. Strong interactions between the Fe ions and polysulfide was suggested useful to address the problem of the diffusion of polysulfide [296]. Moreover, the presence of carbon in the sample ensured an improved electrical conductivity. The use of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}/C$ as a cathode for Li-S batteries allowed a specific capacity of $642\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{h}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at the rate of 1 C for 300 cycles [330].
Recently, it has been articulated that the complex structures of the active material (multishelled hollow structure and yolk shell structure) are likely to exhibit better electrochemical performance as compared to the simple hollow nanostructures [331,332]. However, the synthesis of such complex structure is not an easy task as it requires special treatments while the product yields are also relatively small. Some researchers have proposed the pyrolysis of MOFs with gases or via chemical etching to prepare hollow complex structures to endow desired compositions and tunable properties [55,218,333]. Recently, Zhang and co-workers have reported the pyrolysis of Ni-MOF for the preparation of yolk shell $\mathrm{NiS}_{2}$ /carbon spheres [334] (Fig. 9). The application of $\Nu\mathrm{i}S_{2}/$ carbon spheres as a cathode in a Li-S battery was observed to deliver an initial discharge capacity of $744\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at the current density of 1 C. The system encountered only a small capacity decay $(0.2\%)$ even after 200 cycles of charging/discharging (Fig. 9).
At present, MOFs are being recognized as a novel cathode material for Li-S batteries due to their well-defined porous structure and chemical, mechanical, and thermal stability. However, MOFs suffer from drawbacks of poor inherent electrical conductivity along with the impartation of extra weight of non-active material when utilized as a cathode [335]. To address this issue, many researchers have explored the conversion of MOFs in other materials (e.g., metal oxides and carbonaceous material) that possess the intrinsic properties of porosity despite their transformation into other forms. The benefit of the derived material is an increase in the electrical and electrochemical properties. Also, many researchers have tried to make composites by wrapping graphene sheets or some other conductive material around the MOF to enhance the overall electrical conductivity to realize effective polysulfide storage. Recently, a step to conquer this limit has been taken by jiang and his group [336]. They developed ppy-MOF (MIL-101, MIL-53 and PCN-224) composites to increase the conductivity with the geometrical advantage of MOF. This composite exhibit ppy-MOF compartments which can effectively confine the sulfur [336]. In short, MOFs can efficiently increase the capacity of Li-S batteries by significantly improving the long-term stability and cyclability. However, there is a serious need to study the interactions of polysulfides with different MOF structures to address their limitations in cyclic stability and retention [293,337,338].
## 5. MOFs and their composites as cathode materials for $\mathbf{LiO}_{2}$ batteries
$\mathrm{LiO}_{2}$ batteries work broadly on the same principle as the other lithium-based batteries with the only difference being in the reaction between lithium (contained in the anode) and oxygen (present in the air) to form $\mathrm{LiO}_{2}$ and $\operatorname{Li}_{2}0_{2}$ (Fig. 7) [339,340]. Briefly, the lithium present in the anode moves towards the cathode (by losing an electron), which generally acts as a filter and catalyst to provide pure air and carry out the subsequent reduction. The reduced oxygen reacts with $\mathrm{Li}^{+}$ to form the desired oxide product (and the electrical energy). During the charging cycle, the above reaction proceeds in the reverse direction. $\mathrm{LiO}_{2}$ batteries are well-known for having a high theoretical energy density $\left(3500\mathsf{W}\mathsf{h}\mathsf{k g}^{-1}\right.$ : five times larger than other Li-ion batteries) plus an environmentally benign nature that makes them a promising energy storage system [341,342]. If a significant proportion of their theoretical specific energy (which exceeds that of LIBs) could be realized in practice, $\mathrm{LiO}_{2}$ batteries can resolve the issues associated with existing energy storage devices [12,17,341,343–345]. However, most Liair batteries still suffer from several disadvantages (e.g., sluggish kinetics of the oxygen reduction reaction (ORR)/oxygen evolution reaction (OER), short cycle life (about 100 cycles), low round trip efficiency, poor rate capability, insoluble discharge products $(\mathrm{Li}_{2}0_{2})$ , and the unavoidable formation of side products $\mathrm{(Li}_{2}\mathrm{CO}_{3}$ and LiOH)) [342,346–350].
To resolve the observed limitations, the ORR/OER must be made more efficient for the commercialization of $\operatorname{Li}-0_{2}$ batteries. Recently, the electrocatalysis of ORR was developed to ensure a better oxygen reduction mechanism for $\mathrm{LiO}_{2}$ batteries [351]. These authors demonstrated $\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}/\mathrm{NPCP@rG0}$ hybrids synthesized from the pyrolysis of ZIF-67/rGO composites. The prepared catalyst exhibited a more positive onset potential with a higher limiting current density. Moreover, it was demonstrated that $\mathrm{Co}_{9}\mathrm{S}_{8}/$ ${\mathsf{N P C P@r G O}}$ could tolerate a higher methanol concentration in comparison to the commercialized $20\mathrm{wt\%\Pt/C}$ catalyst [351]. However, the long cyclability of $\operatorname{Li}-0_{2}$ batteries strongly depends on the reversibility of the discharge product ${\mathrm{(Li}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{2}$ or $\mathrm{LiO}_{2}$ ). In the case of irreversible formation of $\operatorname{Li}_{2}0_{2}$ or $\mathrm{LiO}_{2}$ , the Li- $O_{2}$ batteries may face the problems of failure or gradual reduction in their efficiency.
In literature, the employed organic electrolyte (e.g., propylene carbonate, ethylene carbonate, dimethoxyethane (DME), and tertraglyme (TEGDME)) was unsuitable for $\operatorname{Li}-0_{2}$ batteries due to the formation of irreversible side products [352–354]. To address these issues, many researchers are searching for the perfect catalyst materials that are both able to evolve and reduce oxygen during charging/discharging but are also stable against the electrolyte [355–359]. The commonly used catalysts (Pt, Pd, and Au) in batteries contribute to the sluggish kinetics of ORR/OER. Moreover, they are not fully able to reduce the generation of undesirable and intermediate products (e.g., superoxide radicals), which may further attack the cathode and electrolyte [357,360]. Therefore, there is a need to explore alternative catalysts. In addition to the above issues, lithium forms $\mathrm{Li}_{2}0_{2}$ and $\mathrm{LiO}_{2}$ after reacting with oxygen, and these reactions are not easily reversible in nature. Although the application of a high potential (e.g., ${>}4\lor$ ) can resolve this issue of irreversibility to some extent, this action could result in tradeoffs with the coulombic efficiency and cycle life [361,362]. Additionally, $\mathrm{Li}_{2}{\sf O}_{2}$ is insoluble in electrolyte and precipitates in the batteries if it is not properly accommodated. The solution to this problem is the development of a suitable catalyst which both breaks the O-O bond and catalyzes the recovery of the irreversible product to make the reaction reversible. Thus, a good catalyst for Li-air batteries is one with a porous structure for the transportation of reactants and the accommodation of $\operatorname{Li}_{2}0_{2}$ with the ability to make these reactions reversible.[363]
Over the previous decade, various porous carbon materials (e.g., graphene and CNTs) have been reported as catalysts for $\operatorname{Li}-0_{2}$ batteries. However, most of these suffer from poor cyclability and capacity [364,365]. The main drawback of carbon-based materials is that they form $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{CO}_{3}$ upon reaction with the $\mathrm{Li}^{+}$ . The formed $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{CO}_{3}$ was subsequently deposited on the cathode and blocked the path of oxygen and the electrolyte, increasing the overpotential [366,367]. To overcome these shortcomings, the use of MOFs was proposed to offer unique opportunities in synthesizing efficient ORR catalysts and favorable modifications of the structure of carbon/metal oxide-based cathode materials [7,368–371]. The prime advantage of modifying carbon/metal oxides with MOFs is that the metal in these materials increases the net conductivity, whereas the unique structure of MOFs (e.g., micropores and mesopores) provide an efficient pathway for oxygen diffusion as well as the reduction/evolution of reaction sites along with open sites for product deposition.
In this regard, spinel type $\mathrm{\cdot}\mathsf{A B}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{4}$ where A, $\mathtt{B}=$ metal, $\mathbf{\boldsymbol{X}}=$ chalcogen) porous cobalt–manganese oxide nanocubes were derived from Co Mn MOF and employed as catalysts to reduce the potential of the ORR [372]. The as-prepared catalyst electrode exhibited a low overpotential $(200\mathrm{mV})$ and resulted in an average coulombic efficiency of $95\%$ , which was retained even after five charge/discharge cycles. Also, the efficiency was observed to be stable up to 100 cycles with a limited capacity of $500\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at a current density of $0.16\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ . Interestingly, after comparing the above sample with VX-72 carbon electrode, these authors found that their samples were at least three times more stable than the VX72 carbon electrode, which survived only 29 cycles. Along similar lines, hierarchical porous $\mathrm{ZnO/ZnFe_{2}O_{4}/C}$ (ZZFC) nanocages were prepared from Fe(III)-MOF-5-based nanocage templates [373]. Further, the sample was annealed at different temperatures to maximize the number of mesopores with a simultaneous reduction of macropores. By using an optimized pyrolysis process, these authors made the sample primarily mesoporous and conductive while maintaining its octahedral shape. The composite displayed a large increase in the peak current density for both ORR and OER with the good catalytic activity of the ZZFC electrode. These authors further demonstrated that MOF-based nanocages have a lower overpotential with a primary discharge/charge capacity of $11410\mathrm{mAhg}^{-1}$ at a current density of $300\mathrm{mAg}^{-1}$ . Notably, the cyclability increased greatly with a capacity of $5000\mathrm{mAhg}^{-1}$ for 15 cycles at the same current density [373].
Currently, most studies on Li- ${\bf\nabla}\cdot{\bf O}_{2}$ batteries have been conducted under a pure oxygen environment. There are enormous difficulties that hinder the practical application of Li- ${{\bf{\cdot}}}{\bf{0}}_{2}$ batteries in the ambient environment [374,375]. ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ and the moisture from the atmosphere lead to serious parasitic reactions and side products (resulting in the formation of $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{CO}_{3}$ and LiOH), thereby degrading the overall performance of the Li- $.0_{2}$ batteries. A promising method to deal with this problem is to incorporate an oxygen selective membrane so that one can facilitate the maximum insertion of oxygen species with the least amount of other gases and moisture [376–378]. To meet such demand, a mixed matrix membrane (MMM) was derived by incorporating a polydopamine (PDA) coating on a MOF [379]. The used MOF (amine functionalized CAU-1- $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ ${\mathsf{C A U}}=$ Christian-Albrechts-University)) is highly porous and stable having a composition of $\mathrm{\small{[Al_{4}(O H)_{2}(O C H_{3})_{4}(H_{2}N-b d c)_{3}]}},$ $\mathrm{xH}_{2}0$ ) in a matrix of PMMA (poly(methyl methacrylate)). The role of the MOF structure was to provide a sufficiently high specific surface area for the oxygen diffusion. Similarly, PDA was used to decrease the permeation of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ by promoting ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ sorption (enhanced by the presence of OH and $\mathsf{N H}_{2}$ groups in polydopamine molecules). The PMMA was used as a hydrophilic polymer (intrinsic contact angle below $90^{\circ}$ ), which showed a water contact angle (CA) of ${\sim}68^{\circ}$ [380]. Further, prepared samples of MMM (one with PDA and another without PDA) were investigated for the uptake of various gases. The permeations of $0_{2}$ , $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ , and ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ in the MMM battery were 633.2, 328.6, and 143.4 Barrer, respectively. In contrast, they were recorded to be 627.2, 238.9, and 113.5 Barrer for MMM@PDA, respectively where, 1 Barrer $=10^{-10}\frac{\mathrm{cm}^{3}(\mathrm{STP}).\mathrm{cm}}{\mathrm{sec.cm}^{2}.\mathrm{atm}}$ is the unit of gas permeability [381] is defined by $\begin{array}{r}{\mathsf{P}=\frac{q}{A}\frac{t}{\Delta p}.}\end{array}$
Here, q $(\mathsf{c m}^{3}(\mathsf{S T P}){\bullet}\mathsf{s e c}^{-1})$ is the mass flux of gas through a membrane of area A $(\mathsf{c m}^{2})$ and thickness t (cm), under a partial pressure gradient $\Delta{\mathfrak{p}}$ ( $\mathsf{c m}\mathrm{Hg}$ or atm) across the membrane.
The usage of PMMA and PDA decreased the insertion of $_{\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}}$ and ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ (evidenced by the XRD data which showed no peaks of LiOH and $\mathrm{Li}_{2}\mathrm{CO}_{3}\mathrm{.}$ . The battery survived 66 cycles under an ambient atmosphere $30\%$ relative humidity) with a cut-off capacity of $450\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at a current density of $450\mathrm{mAg}^{-1}$ (voltage range of 2–4.6 V) [379].
The supply of $0_{2}$ can also be affected by the nature of the cathode material due to the low interaction of $0_{2}$ with the material or the electrolyte or the product deposited in the pores of the cathode material. An efficient interaction of MOFs with gases can be achieved through the creation of appropriate micro and mesoporosities. Therefore, suitably designed MOFs allow robust interaction of gases within their pores and this offers huge capability for gas storage.
Thus, MOFs can provide a superior oxygen supply for Li-air batteries without significantly increasing the weight and volume. In this regard, three samples (MOF-5 $[Z\mathrm{n}_{4}0(\mathrm{BDC})_{3}$ , $\mathsf{B D C}=1,4-$ benzenedicarboxylate], HKUST-1 $\mathsf{I C u}_{3}(\mathsf{B T C})_{2}$ , BTC $\mathbf{\sigma}=\mathbf{\sigma}$ benzene-1,3,5- tricarboxylate], and M-MOF-74 were tested on a parallel basis [382]. Among them, MOF-5 (with a maximum surface area of $3622\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ (Table 2)) can take up oxygen up to $6.6\:\mathrm{mg}\:\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , giving a capacity of $1780\mathrm{mAhg}^{-1}$ . In contrast, HKUST-1 has $\mathtt{C u(I I)}$ metal cations, which provided binding sites for oxygen and delivered a capacity of $4170\mathrm{mAhg^{-1}}$ by taking up $16.6\mathrm{mg}$ of oxygen per gram. Further, these authors used $40\%$ super P to increase the conductivity of MOFs, and the resulting product showed an increase of 18 times in the concentration of $0_{2}$ at ambient pressure and temperature (273 K). The third sample (M-MOF-74, a series of MOFs built from various divalent metal ions and 2,5- dihydroxyterephthalic acid $\mathrm{'H_{4}D O B D C)}$ with a hexagonal structure and 1D pore structure) was designed to reach a maximum surface area and $0_{2}$ uptake capability. The authors used Co, Mn, and $\mathtt{M g}$ as divalent metal ions. Mg-MOF-74 gave a capacity of $4560\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ with a surface area of $1582\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and oxygen uptake of $15.1\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . These authors recorded the maximum oxygen uptake of $18.4\:\mathrm{mg}\:\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ by Co-MOF-74 with a surface area of $1446\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and a capacity of $3630\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . Among all the tested samples, the highest discharge capacity of $9420\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ was obtained by Mn-MOF-74 (with the lowest surface area of $1213\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ among all the tested MOFs but with a much higher oxygen uptake $15.0\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{g}^{-1}.$ (Fig. 12) [382].
The low conductivity of metal oxides derived from MOFs adversely affects the performance of Li-air batteries. However, further investigations suggested that the conductivity of MOFs can be increased by filling their voids with well dispersed nitrogen/carbon precursors. Keeping this in mind, Xu and Wu et al. (2014) synthesized nitrogen-doped graphene/graphene tube composites using MOFs as a template [357]. These authors prepared Febased catalysts through the heat-treatment of a novel ${\sf N}/{\sf N}$ carbon precursor and dicyandiamide (DCDA) in the presence of iron species. N-Fe-MOF was pyrolyzed at four different temperatures (800, 900, 1000, and $1100^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ to obtain the graphene/graphene nanotubes. Onion-like carbon/iron carbide and carbon nanotubes were obtained at 800 and $900^{\circ}\mathsf C,$ respectively. The sample obtained at $1100^{\circ}{\mathsf{C}}$ lacked a well-defined morphology and had large agglomerated particles. These highly graphitized nanocarbons could provide a robust matrix for hosting the active sites to assist in the ORR as catalysts [383–385]. The cell retained $27\%$ of its capacity after the 50th cycle and was stable up to 16 cycles at a high current density of $400\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ (with discharge voltage rising to 2.80 V). The highest discharge capacity of $5300\mathrm{mAhg}^{-1}$ was obtained at a current density of $50\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ [357].
In summary, the optimized structures of MOFs can be modified to provide uniformly dispersed catalytic active sites and high electronic/ionic conductivity. The combination of these outstanding properties is beneficial to enhance the electrocatalytic performance of $\operatorname{Li}-0_{2}$ batteries. Moreover, the unique structures of bimetallic MOFs can lead to excellent catalytic properties. MOFs and MOF-derived materials with predesigned structures and pore environments can act as both highly efficient non-precious metal cathode catalysts for Li- $.0_{2}$ batteries and as oxygen selective membranes for mass/electron transportation with evenly distributed active sites. As such, they represent a viable air electrode option in $\operatorname{Li}-0_{2}$ batteries. The adjustable pore size and open channels of MOFs are other special properties that are is beneficial for the adsorption of $0_{2}$ . The large surface area could provide enough sites for the storage and transmission of $0_{2}$ , making MOFs highly effective for $0_{2}$ adsorption/storage with practical implications for Li- ${{\bf{\cdot}}}{\bf{0}}_{2}$ battery applications in the ambient environment.
## 6. MOFs and their composites as electrodes for Li-hybrid electrochemical capacitors (Li-HECs)
At present, most daily appliances and scientific and industrial equipment rely heavily on energy storage devices (e.g., batteries and supercapacitors). Therefore, the primary goal of researchers in the field of energy storage devices is to develop a single device that can deliver high energy density with tremendous power whenever needed [386–389]. Moreover, the EDLC (electric double layer capacitor) based supercapacitors suffer from drawbacks of self-discharge characteristics, energy density, and reliability. Research on lithium-ion hybrid electrochemical capacitors (LiHECs), also known as lithium-ion hybrid supercapacitors (LiHSCs) and lithium-ion capacitors (LICs), is one of the key steps that have been taken towards realizing the primary goal. Li-HECs consist of two electrodes, one of which belongs to the LIBs and the other belongs to the supercapacitor. There is intercalation and deintercalation of ${\mathrm{Li}}^{+}$ at the anodic electrode, which resembles the faradic process, whereas the electrolyte accumulates over the cathode surface by fast physical adsorption/desorption process like an electric double layer capacitor [390,391]. Thus, Li-HEC has a very high energy density as compared to the conventional supercapacitors, with a small decrease in the power density (Fig. 7) [392,393]. The commonly used anodic electrodes are oxides and layered structures of the carbon family with facile intercalation/ deintercalation of $\mathrm{Li^{+}}$ . However, another counterpart electrode is generally composed of carbonaceous material with a high surface area to facilitate the maximum absorption of electrolyte. In the last few decades, many researchers have attempted to activate, produce, and/or coat the carbon and rGO with urea, light, or some other techniques for practical applications in supercapacitors, batteries and sensors [394–400]. The Li-HEC research area is recognized to be in its early stage of development [14].
A zinc-based MOF (MOF-5) was first used as a cathode in 2014, which was accompanied by the first use of nanoporous carbon derived from a material with $\mathrm{Li}_{4}\mathrm{Ti}_{5}0_{12}$ as an anode [401]. The fabricated Li-HEC device delivered an energy density of $65\mathrm{W}\mathrm{h}\mathrm{kg}^{-1}$ , which was much more than that of conventional supercapacitors $(20{-}40\mathrm{W}\mathrm{h}\mathrm{kg}^{-1}),$ . Moreover, the energy density of the cell remained at $84\%$ of the initial value even after 10,000 charge/discharge cycles [401]. The rationale behind using $\mathrm{Li}_{4}\mathrm{Ti}_{5}0_{12}$ in LiHEC as an anode was based on its very high theoretical capacity of $175\mathrm{mAhg}^{-1}$ with a flat discharge profile at $1.55\mathrm{V}$ as compared to Li metal [402,403]. Moreover, the spinel structure of $\mathrm{Li}_{4}\mathrm{Ti}_{5}0_{12}$ remained unaffected when $\mathrm{Li}^{+}$ ions directly substituted for the $\mathrm{Ti}^{+}$ on the spinel B-sites [404]. In half cell testing (Li/MOF-DC), an acceptable reversible capacity of $66\mathrm{\mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at a current density of $150\mathrm{mAg}^{-1}$ was obtained (Fig. 13). The performance of the device was however not satisfactory due to its low cyclic stability. The main reason for this shortcoming was attributed to the low $\mathrm{Li}^{+}$ diffusion coefficient and poor electron conductivity of $\mathrm{Li}_{4}\mathrm{Ti}_{5}0_{12}$ , which resisted the cyclic performance and power density of LiHEC [405,406]. On similar lines, Pazhamalai and the co-workers demonstrate the Cu-HCF (hexacyanoferrate) as an intercalative electrode whereas, sodium alginate derived graphitic carbon (GC) as a capacitive electrode was used for Li-HEC [407]. With the presence of heteroatoms in GC, it can provide high value of electric double layer capacitance. The fabricated device delivered a high capacitance of $63.64\mathrm{F}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ along with a high energy density of $42.78\mathrm{W}\mathrm{h}\mathrm{kg}^{-1}$ over a wide voltage window of $2.2\mathrm{V}$ [407].
In another report, Wang and the co-worker have demonstrated the synthesis of $\mathsf{M o S}_{2}$ composite with ZIF derived carbon as a battery like electrode [408]. The same material could also be processed as a capacitor like electrode by subsequent activation of the ZIF-8 derived carbon which further increased its micropores and the effective surface area [73,409]. In a Li-HEC setup, the use of ZIF-8 derived carbon was characterized with a large energy density of $155\mathrm{W}\mathrm{h}\mathrm{kg}^{-1}$ with a high-power density of $20\ensuremath{\mathrm{kW}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{kg}}^{-1}$ . Similarly, the use of different morphological carbon can also enhance the electrochemical performance of the overall device [410].
Moreover, the system was highly stable as only a small capacity loess $(0.0021\%)$ was observed even after 10,000 cycle of charging/ discharging [408]. Likewise, MOF derived nitrogen-doped carbon hollow polyhedral (MOF-NC) was also used in Li-HEC devices as a cathode (Fig. 14) [411]. A hexagonal ${\sf W O}_{3}$ coated with carbon was used as an anode due to its high theoretical capacity of $696\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ [411]. The tunnel-like structure of ${\sf W O}_{3}$ was proposed to sufficiently promote the fast diffusion of ${\mathrm{Li}}^{+}$ ions. In the half cell reaction with Li metal, MOF-NC recorded a reversible capacity of $269.9\mathrm{F}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at a current density of $0.2\mathsf{A g}^{-1}$ [411]. The Li-HEC device was assembled with ${\hslash-}\mathsf{W O}_{3}/\mathsf C$ as an anode and MOF-NC as the cathode. The device showed a high energy density of 159.97 W h $\mathbf{kg}^{-1}$ at a power density of $173.6\mathrm{W}\mathrm{kg}^{-1}$ . The capacity of the Li-HEC retained up to $88.3\%$ of the initial capacity even after 3000 cycles of charging/discharging [411]. Polyoxometalatebased MOF (Cu-BTC) nanocomposites were synthesized with rGO for LIBs (labeled as PMG-3) [412]. These authors showed the hybrid behavior of batteries and supercapacitors with POM-based MOFs and rGO. A superior reversible capacity of $1075\mathrm{mAhg^{-1}}$ was maintained even after 100 cycles with a capacity retention of nearly $100\%$ both at 2000 and $3000\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ for over 400 cycles. The obtained capacity with the rGO containing POM-MOFs surpassed the performance of simple Cu-BTC MOF electrodes (Fig. 15). The combined synergistic effect of POMs, MOFs, and rGO together (e.g., due to the multi-electron redox properties of POMs, high electronic conductivity of rGO, and open frameworks of MOFs) results in the buffering of volume expansion while ensuring good cycling stabilities. Moreover, the XRD data indicate that the original structure of PMG-3 retained its features even after 100 cycles of charging/discharging [412].
Among the various classes of MOFs, ZIF (zeolitic imidazolate frameworks) materials are known for their simple synthesis, high stability, and ease of commercial availability [413–415]. It is also possible to convert ZIFs in to graphitic or highly porous carbon materials with pore sizes in ideal dimensions so as to allow efficient diffusion of electrolytes [73,416–418]. Li et al. have proposed the synthesis of ZIF-8 and MIL-125 derived carbon $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{C}$ composite for potential application in a sodium ion capacitor (NIC) [419]. Different pyrolysis temperature conditions were studied to optimize the surface area and electrochemical performance. The application of the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{C}$ composite material was investigated in a Li-HEC device. This system was operated over a wide voltage widow of $\ensuremath{1-4\mathrm{V}}$ and it delivered a high energy density $(142.7\mathrm{W}\mathrm{h}\mathrm{kg}^{-1};$ and the high power output $(25\mathrm{kW}\mathrm{kg}^{-1}$ ) [419]. A similar device was demonstrated by encapsulating ZIF-67 and ZIF-8 derived carbon into $\mathsf{V O}_{2}$ and $\Nu{\sf a}_{3}\tt V_{2}(P O_{4})_{3}$ [420]. Recently, Jiao et al. have demonstrated a ${\sf N b}_{2}0_{5}@C$ composite with rGO which was obtained as a result of pyrolysis of Nb-MOF [421]. The use of above composite as an anode and activated carbon (AC) as cathode facilitated a high energy density of $71.5\mathrm{W}\mathrm{h}\mathrm{kg}^{-1}$ . Here rGO acted as a conducting path way to allow fast electron transfer as well as a buffer layer to compensate the volume changes in the oxides [421].
Lithium metal suffer from lithium dendrites, which may lead to serious internal short circuit problems and an increase in over potential, which restrict their applications in batteries. Many attempts have been done to stabilize the lithium metal during charging and discharging [422]. Generally, the probability of dendrites formation increases with high rate of charging/discharging. One possible solution for this problem is to use lithium oxides or metal oxides instead of lithium metal, in which $\mathrm{Li}^{+}$ can be easily intercalated. However, the structural stability during lithiation/ delithiation is not guaranteed. In the near future, further investigations should focus on enhancing the structural stability of lithiumbased oxides and metal oxides. Without doubt, MOFs are going to play an important role due to their large surface area, hierarchical porous structure, extra structural stability, and efficient channels for mass transport.
## 7. Performance comparison for Li batteries – a critical perspective
Fossil fuels are the best available sources of energy, and they provide distinctive advantages including inexpensive extraction, easy storability, and shipping to distant places almost anywhere in the world. However, the downside of fossil fuels includes the release of hazardous compounds, thus forcing humanity to seek for alternative sources of green energy [84,423]. This need has catalyzed the production of alternate energy (e.g., nuclear, thermal, and natural sources) along with energy storage systems (batteries, supercapacitors, and hybrid devices) [424–429]. Lithium is the lightest metal on earth, making it the most suitable material for batteries with high energy density and cyclability. However, improvement in LiB performance to meet the maximum possible output is still being investigated with suitable electrode materials and electrolytes. MOFs with highly favorable characteristics have been explored for different lithium batteries and supercapacitors [14,423,424,430,431]. The tailorability in the design of MOFs through the usage of metal and linker combinations can result in an enhanced electrode material with high specific surface area, well defined pore size distribution, and chemical/thermal stabilities [432].
The unique features of MOFs make them highly feasible for use in the types of batteries being considered. LIBs need a well-defined structure to intercalate and deintercalate ${\mathrm{Li}}^{+}$ without any hindrance. The first usage of pristine MOF-177 for LIBs was not very successful due to the lack of interactions between the MOF and ${\mathrm{Li}^{+}}$ [116]. However, the combination of Ni and Li MOF successfully yielded good cyclic stability and capacity. Later, it was found that the MOF linkers can greatly affect the capacity and stability of the batteries. The presence of carboxylic groups in the linkers can significantly enhance the performance. Consequently, BHC (having six carboxylic groups) delivered an enhanced capacity relative to BDC and BTC. Many MOFs, including Cu-BTC, Fe-BDC, CoBTC, and Ni-BHC have been demonstrated to successfully deliver much higher capacities than pristine MOF-177, while still suffering from poor cycle stability (an average value of 200–300 cycles) [116,120,126,135,136,139]. The pristine MOFs generally lacked electrical conductivity or were subject to structure rupturing during charging/discharging cycles. These barriers were however resolved through the synthesis of MOF composites with a conductive medium or introduction of dopants such as nitrogen.
Apart from this, the use of MOF derived materials (oxides and NPCs) are intriguing options to be considered over pristine MOFs in terms of the electrochemical performance [14,345,433]. The reason for this is that the derived oxides with the same surface area and pore size distribution as the parent MOF, are known to store ${\mathrm{Li}^{+}}$ through reversible redox reactions, which is not possible for the pristine MOFs. MOF derived oxides including $\scriptstyle\mathbf{Mn}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{3}$ $(380\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{h}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\cdot$ ), $\mathtt{C u O}$ $(210\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{h}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\mathrm{.}$ ), and $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ $(560\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{h}\mathrm{g}^{-1}),$ yielded much better results than the same oxides synthesized from other conventional methods. Similarly, the derived NPCs retain the surface area of the parent MOF, which is much higher than the commercially available activated carbon. Many common MOFs (e.g., ZIF-8, ZIF-67, MOF-5, and HKUST-1) have been pyrolyzed to acquire the highly porous and conductive NPCs for supercapacitor applications [14,345,434,435]. Further, these MOF derived NPCs can be used as composites with oxides to increase their electrical conductivity and still have the advantage of high surface area.
Li-S batteries are the other lithium-based batteries that have a high specific energy density of $500\mathrm{W}\mathrm{h}\mathrm{kg}^{-1}$ , which is better than the LIBs $\left(300{-}400\mathsf{W}\mathrm{h}\mathrm{kg}^{-1}\right)$ ) [436]. MOFs in Li-S batteries suffer from the existing problems of the dissolution of polysulfide as a product. MOFs with a large pore size distribution and interaction sites with polysulfide can greatly quench the dissolution of product in electrolyte due to the storage of sulfur as an active material [293]. As a result, HKUST-1 was capable of storing large amounts of sulfur, resulting in a capacity of $500\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ over 170 cycles of charging/discharging. One excellent advantage of Li-S batteries over the LIBs is their temperature stability. Li-S batteries are much more stable than LIBs, even at temperatures as low as $-40^{\circ}\mathsf C$ [437]. However, Li- ${{\bf{\cdot}}}_{}0_{2}$ batteries have the highest theoretical capacity and do not require cathodic material inside the batteries. The main challenge in these batteries is the purification of incoming air, and their reduction and evolution on charging/discharging cycles [437]. MOFs with definite window sizes allow only some molecules from air; hence, they have been used in a wide range of applications in gas sensing, storage, and purification [438–440]. The prepared $\mathrm{ZnO/ZnFe_{2}O_{4}/C}$ from MOF nanocages delivered a capacity of ${>}5000\ \mathrm{mA}\mathrm{h}\ \mathbf{g}^{-1}$ but only for 15 cycles, probably due to the fast retention of capacity. MOFs designed for oxygen purification can block incoming molecules with sizes greater than that of oxygen molecules. However, $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ molecules are smaller than oxygen and are thus able to penetrate the MOF layer to produce irreversible products. MOFs combined with PMMA and PDA can effectively decrease the insertion of odd molecules inside the batteries [373].
The role of MOFs for various batteries can be adjusted based on the nature of application. In the case of LIB-based applications, the main role of the MOF is to provide the maximum surface area and interaction sites either through the derived material or attractive linkers (e.g., BDC, BTC, and BHC). However, the main role of MOFs in Li-S batteries is to store the maximum possible amount of sulfur and to diminish the movement of lithium polysulfide into electrolyte (to suppress the shuttle effect of the polysulfide). In the case of Li- ${{\bf{\cdot}}}{\bf{0}}_{2}$ batteries, the catalytic activity of MOFs should be excellent to reduce the oxygen molecules along the most suitable pores for the selective purification of air. In addition, the material should be able to either adsorb the ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ molecules or remove them from the system. As seen from a study of MOFs for lithium batteries, MOFs (including pristine MOFs, their derived structures, and their composites (with graphene, CNTs, metal oxides, PMMA, and PDA)) are excellent materials to provide stability and improve performance in the lithium batteries. It is thus expected that the MOFs will soon be able to meet the technological demands for the commercialization of Li-S and $\operatorname{Li}-0_{2}$ batteries based on the optimization of parameters, including the electrolyte, separator, and current collector.
In recent past, the application of alternate cations, such as sodium ions $(\mathsf{N a}^{+})$ , has also been explored in the area of battery development. Because sodium is a quite abundant element distributed in Earth’s crust, its application presents an exciting advantage in terms of cost effectiveness [441,442]. Moreover, the insertion chemistry of the $\mathsf{N a}^{+}$ ions is almost similar to that of ${\mathrm{Li}^{+}}$ ions. It has been reported that sodium ion batteries (NIBs) can be brought to a zero charge state (i.e., completely drained state) without compromising the loss or damage to the active material. In contrast, LIBs are not recommended to be drained below $30\%$ of their charging capacity [443]. The commercial application of NIBs is mainly faced with the issue of their low energy densities and large space requirements. In this scenario, NIBs should be more attractive in application areas wherein overall weight of the battery system is not a constraint while one can also bear with the associated costs. Recently, Yan’s group have proposed a highperformance ${\mathsf{N a}}^{+}$ ion hybrid electrochemical supercapacitor (SHEC) [419]. These authors utilized MIL-125(Ti) derived $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ /carbon as an anode material, while ZIF-8 derived porous carbon functioned as a cathode. The above SHEC system delivered a high energy density of $142.7\mathrm{W}\mathrm{h}\mathrm{kg}^{-1}$ along with a high power density of $25\mathrm{kW}\mathrm{kg}^{-1}$ [419]. These values were better than that of a similar device prepared with ${\mathrm{Li}}^{+}$ ion technology (energy density of $155\mathrm{W}\mathrm{h}\mathrm{kg}^{-1}$ and power output of $20\ensuremath{\mathrm{kW}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{kg}^{-1}}$ ) [408]. A Codoped amino functionalized Ti-MOF has been used to derive $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{C},$ which was then investigated as a cathode for a NIB [444]. This system delivered a specific capacity of $174\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at a current density of 6 C for 5000 cycles [444].
A ZIF-67/cobalt polydentate composite was also utilized as a precursor for the synthesis of multi shelled cobalt sulfide nanoboxes whose application as an electrode material of a NIB delivered a specific capacity of $438~\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{hg}^{-1}$ at a current density of $0.5\mathsf{A g}^{-1}$ (100 cycles) [445]. Recently, ZIF-67 derived nitrogen doped CNTs (N-CNTs) have been reported as electrocatalyst in sodium air batteries (NABs) [446]. The MOF derived N-CNTs was reported to offer enhanced stability and electrocatalytic activity compared to the more popular option of $\sf P t/C$ [446]. The overall electrochemical performance of ${\mathsf{N a}}^{+}$ ions based batteries can be improved by suitable selections of anode material, air electrodes, and electrolytes [447]. Hence, ${\mathsf{N a}}^{+}$ ion batteries should ultimately be able to compete with ${\mathrm{Li}^{+}}$ technology in terms of energy density and unit weight with the proper technical advancements; for example, the use of hybrid electrode materials.
Apart from sodium, some other cations are also touted as potential replacements of Li [448–451]. MOFs are driving the research in this particular area as well. For instance, a bi-metal MOF/polyacrylonitrile composite has been converted into bimetal (Fe-Co)
embedded nitrogen doped mesoporous carbon. This hybrid carbon was then investigated in a zinc-air battery as a catalyst for ORR and OER [452]. Similarly, the development of ${\mathsf{N a}}^{+}$ and $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{K}}^{+}$ ion batteries has been proposed with the application of MIL-100(Fe) derived sulfur-nitrogen co-doped porous carbon [453]. Guo et al. have employed HKUST- $\cdot1/{\sf C}$ composite as a host material to sulfur in the design of a aluminum-sulfur battery [454]. The proposed ${\sf S@H K U S T/C}$ composite based system retained a specific capacity of $460\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at the current density of $1\mathsf{A}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ after 500 cycles [451].
The above discussed alternative cation systems are reported to function on insertion chemistry quite similar to that of ${\mathrm{Li}^{+}}$ ions. Nonetheless, such alternative systems have still to be optimized further to ensure their practical acceptability. Among the major issues, optimization of cation weight and control over diffusion chemistry are most important ones. Still, options like $Z\mathrm{n}^{+}$ ion batteries suffer from low energy density, while ${\mathsf{N a}}^{+}$ and $\operatorname{K}^{+}$ ion devices encounter undesirably slow charge storage dynamics due to large ionic sizes [455–457].
In a futuristic context, hybrid batteries are also the alternative for the ideal energy storage devices that can provide higher capacity with good recycling stability. A hybrid combination of supercapacitors (SCs) and LIBs can provide a high energy density as well as power density when needed [389,458]. Combining the hybrid batteries with microchips can yield desired control on the demands of specific energy and power outputs for a specific condition. Moreover, the ability to charge the systems in a few seconds makes them suitable for the next generation hybrid automobiles and energy storage systems [459,460]. However, in view of the limited volume of research on hybrid batteries, it is currently not possible to predict their perfect compositions and performance. Nonetheless, an evaluation of different MOF-derived material and their composites suggest their clear significance in the future development of hybrid batteries. This assumption is primarily based on their advantageous material characteristics such as tunable structure, favorable pore size distribution, and high specific surface areas. Moreover, these characteristics of MOFs can also be easily tailored via judicious selection of synthesis conditions and protocols [400,461–463].
In summary, the $\operatorname{Li}-0_{2}$ and the hybrid batteries are considered to be future energy storage systems once their limitations can be worked out. No other types of lithium batteries except $\operatorname{Li}-0_{2}$ can reach the theoretical limit of a specific capacity of $12,000\mathrm{mAh}\ \mathbf{g}^{-1}$ . Cathodes made of Mn-MOF-74, HKUST-1, and MOF-5 (Fe) derived $\mathrm{ZnO/ZnFe_{2}O_{4}/C}$ nanocages can enable the design of Li- ${{\bf{\cdot}}}{\bf{0}}_{2}$ batteries with 1st discharge capacities of 9420, 4170, and $11{,}410\mathrm{mAh}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively [373,382]. However, after a few cycles of charging/discharging, these batteries became unstable and achieved only a small fraction of the 1st discharge specific capacity. Therefore, a low cycle life seems to be an obstacle to their commercialization. Hence, there is an urgent need to direct research efforts to make such batteries stable for prolonged charging/discharging cycles. The detailed description of MOF for LIBs, LiS and $\mathrm{LiO}_{2}$ batteries with their performance given in the Table 3.
## 8. Conclusion and outlook
With the growing demand for efficient energy storage systems, there is an urgent need to develop novel electronic systems. Lead acid and NiMH batteries generally suffer from some severe drawbacks such as low specific capacity, low discharge rate, selfdischarge, irreversibility and low coulombic efficiency. Therefore, lithium-based batteries are thought to be the best suited alternates to replace those earlier options of batteries. Moreover, these alternative batteries with high energy density and specific capacity provide enough power for automobiles to compete with the available fossil fuels without harming the environment. The crucial part of these devices is the electrode and the electrolyte on which the performance (capacity, cycle stability, energy, and power density) of the device largely depends. Thus, the selection of the right material can make them more powerful in terms of their electrochemical performance.
This review was organized to describe the present state of MOFs, their derived units, and composites for different types of lithium batteries. There is no doubt that the MOF structures exhibit excellent initial specific capacity. However, more research is required to evaluate their use in electrodes from a commercial perspective. For instance, it is necessary to establish their usefulness with respect to parameters like high cycle stability, reproducibility, and safety features (protection against lithium dendrites and the thermal runaway).
MOF composites have been recognized as very important materials due to their excellent features (synergistic combination of MOFs and other materials). Yet, the amount of research on the electrode surface and the structural changes of the MOFs during charging/discharging insufficient. Further, relatively little is known about the bonding mechanism between the MOF and the combined material or the distribution of particles in the hybrid structure. Moreover, the morphology and structure of the electrode material is a very crucial parameter for the insertion of ${\mathrm{Li}}^{+}$ , and requires a study focused on morphology control during the synthesis process [471]. Presently, most of the synthesis processes for MOF composites occur at very high temperatures without delicate control of structure and morphology. Hence, such an investigation will make their synthesis more reproducible, which in turn will help facilitate the effective commercialization of the product or device. Apart from these considerations, MOFs are costlier and insulating in nature. Hence, their synthesis protocols must be investigated to modify their structure through their physical properties, including charge transfer mechanisms and electronic conductivity (e.g., graphene has a much higher conductivity than graphite).
The material cost of MOFs needs to be brought down to popularize their actual applications in many areas, including batteries. This issue can be addressed by synthesizing MOFs with cheaper raw materials that are abundant in nature or easily extractable. The use of hybrid solvents in place of expensive organic solutions might also help in cutting down the product cost. Due to their large surface to volume ratios, MOFs can be useful to design low-weight batteries which will indirectly lead to higher specific capacity. Porous MOF materials also provide an exciting opportunity to act as both electrode and separator to eliminate the additional usage of extra separator layer material. The porous nature of MOFs can further be exploited to dope redox active species which can improve the electrochemical performance. Computational methods are often indispensable option to predesign best possible electrode/el ectrolyte-electrode/current collector system, which are crucial in attaining high cycle stability of LIBs. Further, such methods are also useful to predict or optimize the material coating thickness on the electrodes of the battery system. Thick coating of the electrode material may lead to partial wastage of active material with the rise of cost and the weight of the overall device. This is important to save the overuse of material as well as to maintain the least possible device footprint. Homogenous and optimal deposition of MOF layers over electrodes is still a technological challenge as many of the conventional tricks (e.g., dip coating, drop casting, and spin coating) do not necessarily provide control over layer thickness. In this context, researchers can develop high temperature vacuum press or inkjet printing methods.
The selection of suitable linkers or the doping with appropriate electroactive species is a potential area of future research in order to seek the development of MOFs that can bear enhanced electrochemical activity. This is also important to bring much desired improvements in the cycle stability and specific capacity of the nest-generation LIBs. Suitable structural modifications in MOFs are also important with respect to ensuring a better storage of polysulfides and lithium oxide products in Li-S and Li- ${{\bf{\cdot}}}{\bf{0}}_{2}$ batteries. Such optimizations are critical to inhibit the shuttle effect and overpotential limitations faced during charging/discharging. Bimetallic MOFs are considered as potential candidate to design improved LIBs as they can offer the synthesis of electrode materials with high degree of graphitization combined with high porosity.
2-D MOFs are possibly amongst the best materials to support efficient intercalation and de-intercalation of $\mathrm{Li^{+}}$ The intercalation of $\mathrm{Li}^{+}$ in 2-D MOFs has been reported to help in circumventing the problem of generation of irreversible products. Therefore, 2-D MOF should be extremely useful to design batteries with maximum reversibility. In particular, 2-D MOFs with suitable interacting metal clusters and linkers can deliver both stability and high electrochemical performance. Moreover, 2-D MOFs can be used in combination with other 2-D materials, such as metal dichalcogenides (e.g., $\mathsf{W S}_{2}$ , $\mathsf{M o S}_{2}$ , $\mathsf{W T e}_{2}$ , and $\mathsf{M o S e}_{2}.$ , to design novel heterostructures with intriguing features. These suggested 2-D composite layered structures are likely to resolve the issues of supply of pure gas in to the system, need of in-situ catalysts, and the suppression of shuttle effect. MOFs can also be combined with biowaste derived carbon in order to prepared low-cost electrodes. The utilization of biowaste derived carbon is frequently advocated for the preparation of efficient supercapacitor electrodes and should be equally useful for the development of low-cost yet efficient LIBs.
It can be concluded that MOFs and their composites present huge potential in the development of the next-generation efficient LIBs. MOFs have the potential to play multiple roles to cover electrodes, catalysts, purifying membranes, etc. A list of the key technological challenges in the future of lithium-based energy storage systems can be summarized as: (i) elaboration of mechanism of lithium interaction with MOF, (ii) optimization of lithium sites in MOFs, (iii) optimization of cavity size and pore volume of MOFs that can host ionic liquids, (iv) design and synthesis of MOFs with optimum and tunable windows and pore size distribution, and (v) in-depth studies on catalytic activity. There is also a need to evaluate the cost benefit ratio and environmental impacts of MOF based LIBs to maximize their applicabilities in a practical sense.
